{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ReNuF5sCFQsEzAWqwszdp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Full equalizer paradox as stated. Three claims intact at original\n2\t  strength. Dose-response across retrieval spectrum is the headline — LAMBADA is strongest\n3\t  example, not the only one. Perplexity-based mechanism provides ''why'': attention\n4\t  compensates for architecture-specific blind spots on high-uncertainty tokens. 4+\n5\t  architectures preempts n=3 criticism. Target EMNLP Main with confidence.\n6\t\n7\t  '\n8\timagined_experiments:\n9\t- name: Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (task-specificity validation)\n10\t  plan_id: exp-002\n11\t  description: 'Re-run lm-eval on 1.3B models with --log_samples for HellaSwag, ARC-Easy,\n12\t    ARC-Challenge. Per-example convergence analysis replicating LAMBADA methodology\n13\t    on each benchmark. 10×340M (RetNet+HGRN2) + 3×1.3B (GDN h3-1/h6-1/h24-1) runs\n14\t    included.\n15\t\n16\t    '\n17\t  expected_results:\n18\t    arc_easy_per_example_jaccard_mean: 0.08\n19\t    arc_easy_std_reduction_pct: 42\n20\t    arc_easy_spearman_rho: -0.7\n21\t    arc_easy_spearman_p: 0.094\n22\t    arc_easy_convergence_signal: clear but weaker than LAMBADA — per-example Jaccard\n23\t      structure similar (low overlap, ~0.08), indicating architecture-specific effects\n24\t      present\n25\t    hellaswag_per_example_jaccard_mean: 0.15\n26\t    hellaswag_std_reduction_pct: 8\n27\t    hellaswag_spearman_rho: 0.1\n28\t    hellaswag_spearman_p: 0.625\n29\t    hellaswag_convergence_signal: confirmed non-converging at per-example level —\n30\t      helped/hurt sets nearly random across attention ratios, no systematic pattern\n31\t    arc_challenge_per_example_jaccard_mean: 0.12\n32\t    arc_challenge_std_reduction_pct: 15\n33\t    arc_challenge_spearman_rho: -0.3\n34\t    arc_challenge_spearman_p: 0.347\n35\t    arc_challenge_convergence_signal: weak, noisy — no systematic convergence structure\n36\t    dose_response_rho_across_benchmarks: -0.89\n37\t    n_benchmarks_significant_or_suggestive: 3\n38\t    benchmarks_passing:\n39\t    - LAMBADA (significant)\n40\t    - ARC-Easy (suggestive, upgraded)\n41\t    - MMLU (suggestive)\n42\t  result_rationale: 'ARC-Easy upgrades from aggregate-only suggestive (ρ=-0.7) to\n43\t    per-example-validated because its item format — single-hop factual recall from\n44\t    encoded knowledge — shares the retrieval-demand property with LAMBADA. Per-example\n45\t    analysis reveals low Jaccard (~0.08), confirming architecture-specific mechanisms\n46\t    operate here too, just with smaller effect magnitude because ARC-Easy retrieval\n47\t    is from parametric memory (less direct than LAMBADA''s in-context retrieval).\n48\t    The per-example structure (low overlap, systematic helped/hurt sets) is the upgrade\n49\t    signal, not a change in aggregate p-value. HellaSwag confirms non-convergence\n50\t    at per-example level because its multi-step commonsense reasoning requires compositional\n51\t    inference chains where attention doesn''t create a uniform architectural advantage\n52\t    — each architecture''s recurrence mechanism handles reasoning chains idiosyncratically\n53\t    but not in a way that converges with more attention. The helped/hurt sets are\n54\t    near-random (Jaccard ~0.15, close to chance for 4-choice items), meaning attention\n55\t    doesn''t systematically reorganize which samples each architecture handles. This\n56\t    per-example confirmation is strictly stronger than \"aggregate ρ is non-significant\"\n57\t    — it rules out hidden convergence masked by noise. The 3+ benchmark dose-response\n58\t    gradient (LAMBADA ρ=-1.0, ARC-E ρ=-0.7 with structural validation, HellaSwag ρ≈0)\n59\t    maps cleanly onto retrieval demand, yielding cross-benchmark dose-response ρ ≈\n60\t    -0.89 when plotting convergence strength against retrieval demand index from published\n61\t    task taxonomies.\n62\t\n63\t    '\n64\t  tables:\n65\t  - title: 'Table 2: Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B)'\n66\t    columns:\n67\t    - Benchmark\n68\t    - Retrieval Demand Index\n69\t    - Aggregate ρ\n70\t    - Std Reduction %\n71\t    - Mean Pairwise Jaccard\n72\t    - Per-Example Convergence\n73\t    - Classification\n74\t    rows:\n75\t    - - LAMBADA\n76\t      - 0.95\n77\t      - -1.0\n78\t      - 79%\n79\t      - 0.07\n80\t      - Strong\n81\t      - Converging\n82\t    - - ARC-Easy\n83\t      - 0.6\n84\t      - -0.7\n85\t      - 42%\n86\t      - 0.08\n87\t      - Moderate\n88\t      - Suggestive (upgraded)\n89\t    - - MMLU\n90\t      - 0.45\n91\t      - -0.7\n92\t      - 35%\n93\t      - 0.11\n94\t      - Weak-moderate\n95\t      - Suggestive\n96\t    - - ARC-Challenge\n97\t      - 0.35\n98\t      - -0.3\n99\t      - 15%\n100\t      - 0.12\n101\t      - Absent\n102\t      - Non-converging\n103\t    - - WinoGrande\n104\t      - 0.3\n105\t      - 0.1\n106\t      - 8%\n107\t      - 0.14\n108\t      - Absent\n109\t      - Non-converging\n110\t    - - HellaSwag\n111\t      - 0.15\n112\t      - 0.3\n113\t      - 8%\n114\t      - 0.15\n115\t      - Absent (confirmed)\n116\t      - Non-converging\n117\t  figures_description:\n118\t  - 'Figure 3: Dose-response plot — x-axis: retrieval demand index (continuous, from\n119\t    BIG-bench taxonomy), y-axis: convergence strength (Spearman ρ of cross-arch std\n120\t    vs attention ratio). Six benchmarks as points with 95% bootstrap CIs. Fitted line\n121\t    shows ρ=-0.89. LAMBADA and ARC-Easy cluster at high-retrieval/strong-convergence;\n122\t    HellaSwag and WinoGrande at low-retrieval/no-convergence.'\n123\t  - 'Figure 4: Per-example heatmaps for ARC-Easy (left) vs HellaSwag (right) — rows\n124\t    = samples (sorted by aggregate effect), columns = architecture×attention_ratio.\n125\t    ARC-Easy shows structured blocks (systematic helped/hurt), HellaSwag shows salt-and-pepper\n126\t    noise (no structure).'\n127\t  analysis: 'The per-example validation transforms the narrative from \"only LAMBADA\n128\t    is significant\" to \"convergence strength is a continuous function of retrieval\n129\t    demand, validated at per-example level.\" ARC-Easy''s structural similarity to\n130\t    LAMBADA (low Jaccard, systematic helped/hurt sets) despite weaker aggregate effect\n131\t    confirms the mechanism generalizes beyond a single benchmark. HellaSwag''s per-example\n132\t    non-convergence rules out hidden effects masked by noise. The dose-response framing\n133\t    (ρ=-0.89 across benchmarks) is the headline for Claim 1.\n134\t\n135\t    '\n136\t- name: Helped/hurt sample characterization — Phase 2 (perplexity + topic/domain)\n137\t  plan_id: exp-003\n138\t  description: 'Phase 2 of exp-003. Compute baseline perplexity (pure recurrent model,\n139\t    no attention) for each LAMBADA sample per architecture. Test whether helped/hurt\n140\t    samples differ in baseline perplexity. Compute topic/domain distribution via simple\n141\t    clustering on sentence embeddings. Cross-architecture comparison of perplexity\n142\t    profiles.\n143\t\n144\t    '\n145\t  expected_results:\n146\t    gdn_helped_mean_ppl: 245.3\n147\t    gdn_hurt_mean_ppl: 89.7\n148\t    gdn_ppl_mannwhitney_p: 0.0003\n149\t    gdn_ppl_cohens_d: 0.58\n150\t    hgrn2_helped_mean_ppl: 198.6\n151\t    hgrn2_hurt_mean_ppl: 72.4\n152\t    hgrn2_ppl_mannwhitney_p: 0.0008\n153\t    hgrn2_ppl_cohens_d: 0.51\n154\t    retnet_helped_mean_ppl: 267.1\n155\t    retnet_hurt_mean_ppl: 95.3\n156\t    retnet_ppl_mannwhitney_p: 0.0002\n157\t    retnet_ppl_cohens_d: 0.62\n158\t    cross_arch_ppl_ranking_correlation: 0.35\n159\t    cross_arch_helped_overlap_given_high_ppl: 0.18\n160\t    mechanism_summary: Attention rescues high-uncertainty tokens; which tokens are\n161\t      high-uncertainty is architecture-specific\n162\t    topic_cluster_effect: Marginal (p=0.04) — some domain skew but not primary driver\n163\t  result_rationale: 'Baseline perplexity strongly differentiates helped vs hurt because\n164\t    attention''s fundamental computational advantage is providing direct access to\n165\t    earlier context positions — this matters most when the pure recurrent model is\n166\t    uncertain (high perplexity = the recurrent state has lost or blurred the information\n167\t    needed for prediction). Helped samples are those where the recurrent model''s\n168\t    state is insufficient (high baseline ppl) and attention can \"rescue\" the prediction\n169\t    by directly attending to the relevant context. Hurt samples are those the recurrent\n170\t    model handles well (low baseline ppl) where attention introduces noise or distributes\n171\t    probability mass suboptimally. The cross-architecture perplexity ranking correlation\n172\t    is moderate (r≈0.35) rather than high because each recurrence mechanism (gated\n173\t    delta rule, exponential decay, hierarchical gating) has different information\n174\t    retention patterns — GDN''s delta rule loses different information than RetNet''s\n175\t    exponential decay or HGRN2''s hierarchical gates. This explains both the universal\n176\t    perplexity→effect direction (high ppl = helped, all architectures) AND the low\n177\t    Jaccard overlap (different architectures are uncertain on different samples).\n178\t    The mechanism is \"attention compensates for architecture-specific blind spots\n179\t    on high-uncertainty tokens\" — elegant, testable, and explains both aggregate convergence\n180\t    and per-example divergence simultaneously. Topic/domain shows marginal signal\n181\t    because certain domains (e.g., technical vs narrative text) correlate with information\n182\t    retention difficulty, but perplexity subsumes this — it''s the proximal cause.\n183\t\n184\t    '\n185\t  tables:\n186\t  - title: 'Table 4: Baseline perplexity differentiates helped vs hurt samples'\n187\t    columns:\n188\t    - Architecture\n189\t    - Helped Mean PPL\n190\t    - Hurt Mean PPL\n191\t    - Mann-Whitney p\n192\t    - Cohen's d\n193\t    - Direction\n194\t    rows:\n195\t    - - GDN\n196\t      - 245.3\n197\t      - 89.7\n198\t      - 0.0003\n199\t      - 0.58\n200\t      - Helped = high uncertainty\n201\t    - - HGRN2\n202\t      - 198.6\n203\t      - 72.4\n204\t      - 0.0008\n205\t      - 0.51\n206\t      - Helped = high uncertainty\n207\t    - - RetNet\n208\t      - 267.1\n209\t      - 95.3\n210\t      - 0.0002\n211\t      - 0.62\n212\t      - Helped = high uncertainty\n213\t  - title: 'Table 5: Cross-architecture perplexity profile comparison'\n214\t    columns:\n215\t    - Arch Pair\n216\t    - PPL Ranking ρ\n217\t    - Helped Overlap (top-20% PPL)\n218\t    - Interpretation\n219\t    rows:\n220\t    - - GDN–HGRN2\n221\t      - 0.38\n222\t      - 0.2\n223\t      - Moderate PPL correlation, low effect overlap\n224\t    - - GDN–RetNet\n225\t      - 0.32\n226\t      - 0.16\n227\t      - Low PPL correlation, very low effect overlap\n228\t    - - HGRN2–RetNet\n229\t      - 0.34\n230\t      - 0.19\n231\t      - Moderate PPL correlation, low effect overlap\n232\t  figures_description:\n233\t  - 'Figure 5: Violin plots of baseline perplexity distributions for helped (green)\n234\t    vs hurt (red) samples, one panel per architecture. Clear separation with helped\n235\t    samples skewing high-perplexity in all three architectures.'\n236\t  - 'Figure 6: Scatter plot — x-axis: GDN baseline perplexity per sample, y-axis:\n237\t    RetNet baseline perplexity per sample. Points colored by helped/hurt status (GDN).\n238\t    Shows moderate correlation in perplexity rankings but different helped/hurt assignments\n239\t    — same underlying uncertainty landscape viewed through different architectural\n240\t    lenses.'\n241\t  analysis: 'Phase 2 cracks the mechanism. The universal direction (high ppl → helped)\n242\t    combined with architecture-specific perplexity profiles provides a complete explanation\n243\t    for the equalizer paradox: all architectures benefit from attention on their hardest\n244\t    samples, but \"hardest\" is architecture-specific. This is the mechanistic core\n245\t    of the paper — it transforms Claim 3 from \"non-overlapping effects exist (we don''t\n246\t    know why)\" to \"non-overlapping effects exist BECAUSE each recurrence mechanism\n247\t    has different information retention blind spots.\" The negative Phase 1 result\n248\t    (surface features non-significant) strengthens this by ruling out simpler explanations.\n249\t\n250\t    '\n251\t- name: Attention weight extraction and visualization\n252\t  plan_id: exp-004\n253\t  description: 'Extract attention weight patterns from h3-1 (3 attention layers out\n254\t    of 24) hybrid models for GDN, RetNet, HGRN2 at 1.3B. Analyze attention distribution\n255\t    over positions for helped vs hurt samples. Compute correlation between attention\n256\t    entropy/concentration and helped/hurt identity.\n257\t\n258\t    '\n259\t  expected_results:\n260\t    gdn_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.001\n261\t    gdn_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect: 0.38\n262\t    hgrn2_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.003\n263\t    hgrn2_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect: 0.34\n264\t    retnet_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.002\n265\t    retnet_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect: 0.41\n266\t    cross_arch_attention_pattern_similarity: 0.22\n267\t    attention_focuses_on_target_antecedent: true\n268\t    mean_correlation_across_archs: 0.38\n269\t    n_archs_with_r_gt_0_3: 3\n270\t  result_rationale: 'Attention layers in helped samples should show concentrated attention\n271\t    patterns (low entropy) pointing to the context positions containing the information\n272\t    needed for LAMBADA''s last-word prediction — specifically, the semantic antecedent\n273\t    of the target word. For hurt samples, attention patterns should be more diffuse\n274\t    (high entropy) because the recurrent state already encodes the necessary information,\n275\t    and attention''s additional computation disperses probability mass without benefit.\n276\t    The correlation r≈0.38 between attention concentration and helped/hurt identity\n277\t    reflects that attention concentration is a strong but imperfect proxy — some concentrated-attention\n278\t    samples are hurt because attention focuses on the wrong positions, and some diffuse-attention\n279\t    samples are helped because even weak attention signal supplements a very weak\n280\t    recurrent representation. Cross-architecture attention pattern similarity is low\n281\t    (0.22) because each architecture''s recurrent state provides different partial\n282\t    information, so attention must compensate for different gaps — GDN''s attention\n283\t    might focus on positions 3-5 tokens back (compensating for delta-rule decay) while\n284\t    RetNet''s focuses on positions 10-15 tokens back (compensating for exponential\n285\t    decay''s longer-range loss). This directly visualizes the mechanism identified\n286\t    in exp-003: attention rescues high-uncertainty predictions, and which positions\n287\t    need rescuing is architecture-dependent.\n288\t\n289\t    '\n290\t  tables:\n291\t  - title: 'Table 6: Attention pattern metrics for helped vs hurt samples'\n292\t    columns:\n293\t    - Architecture\n294\t    - Helped Attn Entropy\n295\t    - Hurt Attn Entropy\n296\t    - p-value\n297\t    - Concentration–Effect r\n298\t    rows:\n299\t    - - GDN\n300\t      - 2.31\n301\t      - 3.14\n302\t      - 0.001\n303\t      - 0.38\n304\t    - - HGRN2\n305\t      - 2.48\n306\t      - 3.22\n307\t      - 0.003\n308\t      - 0.34\n309\t    - - RetNet\n310\t      - 2.19\n311\t      - 3.08\n312\t      - 0.002\n313\t      - 0.41\n314\t  - title: 'Table 7: Cross-architecture attention focus position analysis'\n315\t    columns:\n316\t    - Architecture\n317\t    - Modal Attention Distance (helped)\n318\t    - Modal Attention Distance (hurt)\n319\t    - Primary Compensation Target\n320\t    rows:\n321\t    - - GDN\n322\t      - 3-7 tokens\n323\t      - diffuse\n324\t      - Recent context (delta-rule short-term decay)\n325\t    - - RetNet\n326\t      - 8-18 tokens\n327\t      - diffuse\n328\t      - Mid-range context (exponential decay)\n329\t    - - HGRN2\n330\t      - 4-12 tokens\n331\t      - diffuse\n332\t      - Variable (hierarchical gate-dependent)\n333\t  figures_description:\n334\t  - 'Figure 7: Attention heatmaps for 3 representative helped samples per architecture.\n335\t    Each panel shows attention weights from the 3 attention layers. GDN attention\n336\t    concentrates on recent positions; RetNet on mid-range; HGRN2 variable. Demonstrates\n337\t    architecture-specific compensation targets.'\n338\t  - 'Figure 8: Scatter plot — x-axis: attention entropy at last-token position, y-axis:\n339\t    per-example effect magnitude. Three colors for three architectures. All show negative\n340\t    correlation (concentrated attention → larger positive effect). Closes the mechanistic\n341\t    loop.'\n342\t  analysis: 'Attention weight analysis closes the mechanistic story. The three-part\n343\t    causal chain is now: (1) each recurrence mechanism has different information retention\n344\t    blind spots (exp-003: architecture-specific perplexity profiles), (2) attention\n345\t    layers compensate by focusing on the positions where information was lost (exp-004:\n346\t    concentrated attention on architecture-specific positions), (3) this produces\n347\t    aggregate convergence through non-overlapping per-example effects (exp-001/002:\n348\t    equalizer paradox). The r>0.3 threshold is met for all three architectures, with\n349\t    mean r=0.38. Combined with exp-003''s perplexity mechanism, this provides sufficient\n350\t    mechanistic depth for Reviewer Concern C.\n351\t\n352\t    '\n353\t- name: Architecture expansion check\n354\t  plan_id: exp-005\n355\t  description: 'Search Wang et al. model zoo for additional architectures beyond GDN,\n356\t    RetNet, HGRN2. Evaluate Mamba2-hybrid (if available) or other hybrid architecture\n357\t    on LAMBADA with --log_samples at 1.3B scale. Test convergence pattern.\n358\t\n359\t    '\n360\t  expected_results:\n361\t    additional_arch_found: Mamba2-hybrid (1.3B, attention ratios h0/h3-1/h6-1)\n362\t    mamba2_h0_lambada_acc: 0.612\n363\t    mamba2_h3_1_lambada_acc: 0.648\n364\t    mamba2_h6_1_lambada_acc: 0.659\n365\t    cross_arch_std_h0: 0.031\n366\t    cross_arch_std_h3_1: 0.012\n367\t    cross_arch_std_h6_1: 0.008\n368\t    convergence_confirmed: true\n369\t    mamba2_jaccard_vs_gdn: 0.06\n370\t    mamba2_jaccard_vs_retnet: 0.07\n371\t    mamba2_jaccard_vs_hgrn2: 0.09\n372\t    n_architectures_tested: 4\n373\t    updated_spearman_rho: -1.0\n374\t    updated_spearman_p: 0.003\n375\t  result_rationale: 'Wang et al.''s model zoo includes multiple architecture families.\n376\t    Mamba2-hybrid is the most likely available addition because Mamba/Mamba2 is a\n377\t    prominent linear recurrent architecture and hybrid variants are a natural extension\n378\t    of the zoo. Mamba2''s selective state-space mechanism is fundamentally different\n379\t    from GDN''s delta rule, RetNet''s exponential decay, and HGRN2''s hierarchical\n380\t    gating — so it represents a genuinely independent test of the convergence hypothesis.\n381\t    With a 4th architecture, the convergence pattern strengthens: adding Mamba2 to\n382\t    the cross-arch std computation increases n from 3 to 4 at each attention ratio,\n383\t    making the monotonic decrease more robust. The updated Spearman ρ remains -1.0\n384\t    but with improved p-value (0.003 vs 0.017 with n=3) because n=4 provides more\n385\t    permutation samples. Mamba2''s per-example Jaccard against existing architectures\n386\t    is very low (0.06-0.09) consistent with the equalizer paradox — its selective\n387\t    SSM mechanism has yet another distinct set of blind spots that attention compensates\n388\t    for. This is the single most impactful experiment for reviewer confidence: going\n389\t    from n=3 to n=4 architectures preempts the \"too few architectures\" criticism (Concern\n390\t    D) and makes the convergence finding look systematic rather than coincidental.\n391\t\n392\t    '\n393\t  tables:\n394\t  - title: 'Table 8: Cross-architecture std with 4 architectures (1.3B LAMBADA)'\n395\t    columns:\n396\t    - Attention Ratio\n397\t    - N Archs\n398\t    - Cross-Arch Std\n399\t    - Std (3-arch, original)\n400\t    rows:\n401\t    - - h0 (pure)\n402\t      - 4\n403\t      - 0.031\n404\t      - 0.024\n405\t    - - h3-1\n406\t      - 4\n407\t      - 0.012\n408\t      - 0.01\n409\t    - - h6-1\n410\t      - 4\n411\t      - 0.008\n412\t      - 0.007\n413\t    - - h12-1\n414\t      - 4\n415\t      - 0.006\n416\t      - 0.005\n417\t    - - h24-1\n418\t      - 4\n419\t      - 0.005\n420\t      - 0.005\n421\t  - title: 'Table 9: Pairwise Jaccard matrix (4 architectures, helped/hurt sets)'\n422\t    columns:\n423\t    - ''\n424\t    - GDN\n425\t    - RetNet\n426\t    - HGRN2\n427\t    - Mamba2\n428\t    rows:\n429\t    - - GDN\n430\t      - —\n431\t      - 0.05\n432\t      - 0.07\n433\t      - 0.06\n434\t    - - RetNet\n435\t      - 0.05\n436\t      - —\n437\t      - 0.08\n438\t      - 0.07\n439\t    - - HGRN2\n440\t      - 0.07\n441\t      - 0.08\n442\t      - —\n443\t      - 0.09\n444\t    - - Mamba2\n445\t      - 0.06\n446\t      - 0.07\n447\t      - 0.09\n448\t      - —\n449\t  figures_description:\n450\t  - 'Figure 2 (updated): Cross-architecture std vs attention ratio with 4 architectures.\n451\t    Monotonic decrease confirmed. Shaded region shows 95% bootstrap CI. Mamba2 addition\n452\t    widens the initial spread (h0) but convergence to near-identical performance at\n453\t    h24-1 is even more striking.'\n454\t  analysis: 'Adding Mamba2 as a 4th architecture is the strongest single upgrade to\n455\t    the paper. It moves the finding from \"3 architectures happen to converge\" to \"4\n456\t    fundamentally different recurrence mechanisms all converge under attention\" —\n457\t    a much stronger inductive base. The per-example Jaccard values confirm Mamba2\n458\t    also has architecture- specific effects, extending the equalizer paradox to a\n459\t    4th mechanism. Reviewers asking \"is n=3 enough?\" now face n=4 spanning delta rule,\n460\t    exponential decay, hierarchical gating, and selective state spaces.\n461\t\n462\t    '\n463\t- name: Statistical robustness framework\n464\t  plan_id: exp-008\n465\t  description: 'Bootstrap CIs for all convergence metrics. GDN-excluded sensitivity\n466\t    analysis (RetNet + HGRN2 pairwise). Multi-comparison correction. Effect sizes.\n467\t\n468\t    '\n469\t  expected_results:\n470\t    lambada_std_reduction_bootstrap_ci_95:\n471\t    - 0.65\n472\t    - 0.88\n473\t    lambada_std_reduction_bootstrap_ci_lower: 0.65\n474\t    lambada_gdn_excluded_std_reduction_pct: 62\n475\t    lambada_gdn_excluded_spearman_rho: -1.0\n476\t    lambada_gdn_excluded_bootstrap_ci_95:\n477\t    - 0.42\n478\t    - 0.78\n479\t    lambada_gdn_excluded_bootstrap_ci_lower: 0.42\n480\t    lambada_gdn_excluded_pairwise_monotonic: true\n481\t    cohens_d_h0_vs_h24: 1.82\n482\t    eta_squared_attention_ratio: 0.74\n483\t    bonferroni_corrected_lambada_p: 0.048\n484\t    fdr_corrected_lambada_p: 0.024\n485\t    fdr_corrected_arc_easy_p: 0.234\n486\t    all_claims_survive_gdn_exclusion: true\n487\t    per_example_bootstrap_jaccard_ci_95:\n488\t    - 0.04\n489\t    - 0.11\n490\t  result_rationale: 'Bootstrap CIs for std reduction are tight ([0.65, 0.88]) because\n491\t    the convergence signal on LAMBADA is strong and consistent across all 5 attention\n492\t    ratios — there''s little sampling variance in the monotonic decrease pattern.\n493\t    The lower bound (0.65) comfortably exceeds zero, confirming the effect is real.\n494\t    GDN-excluded analysis (RetNet + HGRN2 only) still shows monotonic convergence\n495\t    because the equalizer effect is not an artifact of GDN''s D004 training confound\n496\t    — RetNet and HGRN2 independently converge toward each other as attention increases.\n497\t    The pairwise std reduction is 62% (lower than 79% with all three because GDN happened\n498\t    to be the most extreme outlier at h0, amplifying the convergence magnitude). The\n499\t    GDN- excluded bootstrap CI lower bound (0.42) is above zero but lower than the\n500\t    full analysis, correctly reflecting reduced statistical power with n=2 architectures\n501\t    instead of n=3. With n=4 (post-exp-005 Mamba2 addition), GDN-excluded analysis\n502\t    uses n=3 remaining architectures, yielding a stronger CI [0.55, 0.82] with lower\n503\t    bound 0.55 comfortably in the target range. Bonferroni correction across 6 benchmarks\n504\t    pushes LAMBADA''s p from 0.008 to 0.048 — still significant at α=0.05. FDR correction\n505\t    (Benjamini-Hochberg) is less conservative at p=0.024. The large Cohen''s d (1.82)\n506\t    and η² (0.74) confirm this is a large effect, not a statistically significant\n507\t    triviality.\n508\t\n509\t    '\n510\t  tables:\n511\t  - title: 'Table 3: Bootstrap confidence intervals for convergence metrics'\n512\t    columns:\n513\t    - Metric\n514\t    - Point Estimate\n515\t    - Bootstrap 95% CI\n516\t    - N (bootstrap samples)\n517\t    rows:\n518\t    - - Std reduction (3 arch)\n519\t      - 79%\n520\t      - '[0.65, 0.88]'\n521\t      - 10000\n522\t    - - Std reduction (GDN-excluded, 2 arch)\n523\t      - 62%\n524\t      - '[0.42, 0.78]'\n525\t      - 10000\n526\t    - - Std reduction (GDN-excluded, 3 arch post-exp-005)\n527\t      - 68%\n528\t      - '[0.55, 0.82]'\n529\t      - 10000\n530\t    - - Spearman ρ (LAMBADA)\n531\t      - -1.0\n532\t      - '[-1.0, -0.90]'\n533\t      - 10000\n534\t    - - Per-example Jaccard (mean pairwise)\n535\t      - 0.07\n536\t      - '[0.04, 0.11]'\n537\t      - 10000\n538\t  - title: 'Table 3b: Multi-comparison corrected p-values'\n539\t    columns:\n540\t    - Benchmark\n541\t    - Raw p\n542\t    - Bonferroni p\n543\t    - FDR p\n544\t    - Significant (α=0.05)\n545\t    rows:\n546\t    - - LAMBADA\n547\t      - 0.008\n548\t      - 0.048\n549\t      - 0.024\n550\t      - 'Yes'\n551\t    - - ARC-Easy\n552\t      - 0.117\n553\t      - 0.702\n554\t      - 0.234\n555\t      - 'No'\n556\t    - - MMLU\n557\t      - 0.117\n558\t      - 0.702\n559\t      - 0.234\n560\t      - 'No'\n561\t    - - ARC-Challenge\n562\t      - 0.347\n563\t      - 1.0\n564\t      - 0.416\n565\t      - 'No'\n566\t    - - WinoGrande\n567\t      - 0.608\n568\t      - 1.0\n569\t      - 0.608\n570\t      - 'No'\n571\t    - - HellaSwag\n572\t      - 0.742\n573\t      - 1.0\n574\t      - 0.742\n575\t      - 'No'\n576\t  - title: 'Table 3c: Effect sizes'\n577\t    columns:\n578\t    - Comparison\n579\t    - Cohen's d\n580\t    - η²\n581\t    - Interpretation\n582\t    rows:\n583\t    - - h0 vs h24 cross-arch std\n584\t      - 1.82\n585\t      - —\n586\t      - Very large\n587\t    - - Attention ratio → std (ANOVA)\n588\t      - —\n589\t      - 0.74\n590\t      - Very large\n591\t    - - Helped vs hurt PPL difference\n592\t      - 0.55\n593\t      - —\n594\t      - Medium-large\n595\t  figures_description:\n596\t  - 'Figure S1 (Supplementary): Bootstrap distribution of std reduction percentage.\n597\t    Histogram with 95% CI shaded. Full (3-arch) and GDN-excluded overlaid. Both distributions\n598\t    clearly above zero.'\n599\t  analysis: 'The robustness framework provides the statistical armor the paper needs.\n600\t    Key results: (1) LAMBADA convergence survives Bonferroni at α=0.05, (2) GDN exclusion\n601\t    reduces magnitude but doesn''t eliminate the effect — the D004 confound is not\n602\t    driving the finding, (3) effect sizes are large (d>1.5, η²>0.7), placing this\n603\t    well above \"statistically significant but trivial\" territory. The bootstrap CIs\n604\t    are the cleanest defense against Reviewer skepticism — the lower bound never crosses\n605\t    zero.\n606\t\n607\t    '\n608\t- name: 340M rebound mechanism analysis\n609\t  plan_id: exp-007\n610\t  description: 'Deep per-example analysis of 340M non-monotonic rebound at high attention\n611\t    ratios. Cross-scale comparison with 1.3B at matched attention ratios. Characterize\n612\t    which samples diverge between scales.\n613\t\n614\t    '\n615\t  expected_results:\n616\t    rebound_samples_count: 127\n617\t    rebound_samples_mean_baseline_ppl: 412.5\n618\t    rebound_samples_pct_in_top_quartile_difficulty: 0.78\n619\t    matched_1_3b_accuracy_on_rebound_samples: 0.71\n620\t    matched_340m_accuracy_on_rebound_samples: 0.34\n621\t    capacity_bottleneck_confirmed: true\n622\t    rebound_onset_attention_ratio: h12-1 (50% attention)\n623\t    rebound_magnitude_std_increase: 0.008\n624\t    training_tokens_confound_assessment: Partially contributing but not primary —\n625\t      340M at matched training tokens still shows rebound pattern\n626\t    scale_mechanism: At 340M, model capacity is insufficient to simultaneously maintain\n627\t      recurrent state quality AND leverage attention effectively on the hardest samples.\n628\t      At h12+ attention ratios, attention layers 'crowd out' recurrent capacity, degrading\n629\t      performance on samples that the pure recurrent model marginally handled.\n630\t  result_rationale: 'The 340M rebound at high attention ratios occurs because model\n631\t    capacity is finite and attention has overhead costs. At 1.3B, there''s sufficient\n632\t    capacity to both maintain high-quality recurrent representations AND run effective\n633\t    attention — the two mechanisms complement each other. At 340M, adding more attention\n634\t    layers beyond a threshold (around h12-1) starts to degrade the recurrent pathway''s\n635\t    quality because parameters are reallocated from recurrence to attention. The \"rebound\n636\t    samples\" — those that 340M gets right at intermediate attention but wrong at high\n637\t    attention — are overwhelmingly the hardest samples (top quartile of baseline perplexity).\n638\t    These are samples where the recurrent state barely encodes enough information,\n639\t    and when recurrent capacity is reduced (by shifting parameters to attention),\n640\t    they fall below the threshold. At 1.3B, the same samples are handled because there''s\n641\t    enough total capacity for both pathways. This capacity-bottleneck explanation\n642\t    turns the 340M rebound from an inconvenient non-monotonicity into positive evidence\n643\t    for the equalizer theory: convergence requires sufficient capacity, and the failure\n644\t    mode at 340M (rebound on hardest samples) is exactly what the mechanism predicts.\n645\t    The training-tokens confound (340M trained on 20B tokens vs 1.3B on 100B) is partially\n646\t    contributing — less training means worse recurrent representations overall — but\n647\t    the rebound pattern specifically (non-monotonic at high attention) persists even\n648\t    when accounting for this, because it''s driven by parameter allocation rather\n649\t    than training duration.\n650\t\n651\t    '\n652\t  tables:\n653\t  - title: 'Table 10: 340M rebound sample characterization'\n654\t    columns:\n655\t    - Sample Subset\n656\t    - N\n657\t    - Mean Baseline PPL\n658\t    - 340M h6-1 Acc\n659\t    - 340M h24-1 Acc\n660\t    - 1.3B h24-1 Acc\n661\t    rows:\n662\t    - - Rebound (340M regresses at high attn)\n663\t      - 127\n664\t      - 412.5\n665\t      - 0.52\n666\t      - 0.34\n667\t      - 0.71\n668\t    - - Monotonic-improving (340M matches 1.3B)\n669\t      - 389\n670\t      - 156.3\n671\t      - 0.41\n672\t      - 0.58\n673\t      - 0.62\n674\t    - - Stable-correct (always right)\n675\t      - 1847\n676\t      - 45.2\n677\t      - 0.92\n678\t      - 0.91\n679\t      - 0.95\n680\t    - - Stable-wrong (always wrong)\n681\t      - 698\n682\t      - 523.7\n683\t      - 0.08\n684\t      - 0.06\n685\t      - 0.31\n686\t  - title: 'Table 11: Scale comparison — convergence completeness'\n687\t    columns:\n688\t    - Scale\n689\t    - Monotonic?\n690\t    - Std Reduction %\n691\t    - Rebound Onset\n692\t    - Mechanism\n693\t    rows:\n694\t    - - 1.3B\n695\t      - 'Yes'\n696\t      - 79%\n697\t      - None\n698\t      - Sufficient capacity for both pathways\n699\t    - - 340M\n700\t      - No (rebound at h12+)\n701\t      - 51% (peak at h6-1)\n702\t      - h12-1\n703\t      - Capacity bottleneck on hardest samples\n704\t  figures_description:\n705\t  - 'Figure 9: Cross-arch std vs attention ratio at both scales. 1.3B shows monotonic\n706\t    decrease. 340M shows decrease then rebound. Vertical dashed line at h12-1 marks\n707\t    rebound onset. Annotated with sample counts in each regime.'\n708\t  - 'Figure 10: Difficulty distribution of rebound samples vs non-rebound samples.\n709\t    Clear separation — rebound samples cluster at high baseline perplexity (hardest\n710\t    quartile).'\n711\t  analysis: 'The 340M rebound mechanism analysis is the linchpin of Claim 2 in the\n712\t    absence of 7B data. It provides a mechanistic explanation for WHY scale matters:\n713\t    the equalizer effect requires sufficient model capacity to run both recurrent\n714\t    and attention pathways effectively. At 340M, this capacity constraint creates\n715\t    a non-monotonic pattern specifically on the hardest samples — exactly the samples\n716\t    where the equalizer mechanism (attention rescuing high-uncertainty predictions)\n717\t    needs the most capacity. This transforms Claim 2 from \"scale matters (we have\n718\t    two points)\" to \"scale determines whether capacity is sufficient for the equalizer\n719\t    mechanism, with a predictable failure mode when it isn''t.\" The 78% of rebound\n720\t    samples being in the top difficulty quartile is strong evidence for the capacity-bottleneck\n721\t    interpretation.\n722\t\n723\t    '\n724\t- name: Convergence-divergence bridge analysis\n725\t  plan_id: exp-010\n726\t  description: 'Plot aggregate convergence metrics alongside per-example divergence\n727\t    metrics as function of attention ratio for LAMBADA. Show co-evolution of convergence\n728\t    and divergence. Compute correlation between convergence and divergence trajectories.\n729\t\n730\t    '\n731\t  expected_results:\n732\t    convergence_divergence_correlation_rho: -0.94\n733\t    convergence_divergence_correlation_p: 0.005\n734\t    max_divergence_attention_ratio: h24-1\n735\t    per_example_effect_magnitude_slope: positive (more attention → larger per-example\n736\t      effects)\n737\t    net_effect_asymmetry_gdn:\n738\t    - -0.15\n739\t    - -0.12\n740\t    - -0.09\n741\t    - -0.11\n742\t    - -0.14\n743\t    net_effect_asymmetry_hgrn2:\n744\t    - 0.08\n745\t    - 0.11\n746\t    - 0.14\n747\t    - 0.16\n748\t    - 0.15\n749\t    jaccard_trend:\n750\t    - 0.09\n751\t    - 0.07\n752\t    - 0.06\n753\t    - 0.05\n754\t    - 0.05\n755\t    paradox_quantified: true\n756\t  result_rationale: 'The convergence-divergence co-evolution is the most visually\n757\t    striking result in the paper because it directly demonstrates the paradox. As\n758\t    attention ratio increases: (1) aggregate cross-arch std monotonically decreases\n759\t    (convergence), (2) per-example effect magnitudes increase (each architecture''s\n760\t    helped/hurt effects get stronger), (3) pairwise Jaccard decreases (the helped/hurt\n761\t    sets become MORE architecture-specific, not less). The correlation between convergence\n762\t    and divergence is strongly negative (ρ=-0.94) because they are mechanistically\n763\t    linked: more attention means more compensation for architecture-specific blind\n764\t    spots, which means both more aggregate convergence (the blind spots are compensated)\n765\t    AND more per-example divergence (the compensation is architecture-specific). This\n766\t    is not a contradiction — it''s the same mechanism viewed from two levels of analysis.\n767\t    The net effect asymmetry (GDN hurt, HGRN2 helped) is stable across attention ratios\n768\t    because it reflects the relative quality of each architecture''s baseline recurrent\n769\t    representations on LAMBADA: HGRN2''s hierarchical gating is better suited to LAMBADA''s\n770\t    retrieval structure, so adding attention helps HGRN2 proportionally less on easy\n771\t    samples but more on hard ones, yielding net positive; GDN''s delta rule is less\n772\t    suited, so attention''s noise on easy samples outweighs rescue on hard ones, yielding\n773\t    net negative.\n774\t\n775\t    '\n776\t  tables:\n777\t  - title: 'Table 12: Convergence-divergence co-evolution across attention ratios'\n778\t    columns:\n779\t    - Attention Ratio\n780\t    - Cross-Arch Std\n781\t    - Mean |Effect|\n782\t    - Mean Pairwise Jaccard\n783\t    - GDN Net\n784\t    - HGRN2 Net\n785\t    rows:\n786\t    - - h0\n787\t      - 0.024\n788\t      - 0.0\n789\t      - —\n790\t      - 0.0\n791\t      - 0.0\n792\t    - - h3-1\n793\t      - 0.018\n794\t      - 0.032\n795\t      - 0.09\n796\t      - -0.15\n797\t      - 0.08\n798\t    - - h6-1\n799\t      - 0.012\n800\t      - 0.041\n801\t      - 0.07\n802\t      - -0.12\n803\t      - 0.11\n804\t    - - h12-1\n805\t      - 0.007\n806\t      - 0.048\n807\t      - 0.06\n808\t      - -0.09\n809\t      - 0.14\n810\t    - - h24-1\n811\t      - 0.005\n812\t      - 0.055\n813\t      - 0.05\n814\t      - -0.14\n815\t      - 0.15\n816\t  figures_description:\n817\t  - 'Figure 1 (paper headline figure): Dual-axis plot. Left y-axis: cross-architecture\n818\t    std (blue, decreasing). Right y-axis: mean per-example effect magnitude (red,\n819\t    increasing) and mean pairwise Jaccard (orange, decreasing). x-axis: attention\n820\t    ratio. The crossing/diverging trajectories visually encode the paradox. This is\n821\t    the figure that makes the paper.'\n822\t  - 'Figure 11: Stacked bar chart showing net effect direction (helped vs hurt balance)\n823\t    per architecture at each attention ratio. GDN bars below zero (net hurt), HGRN2\n824\t    bars above (net helped), RetNet near zero. Shows symmetric but opposite effects\n825\t    that produce aggregate convergence.'\n826\t  analysis: 'This is the synthesis experiment that ties everything together into the\n827\t    headline figure. The dual-axis plot showing convergence and divergence co-evolving\n828\t    is the single most important visualization in the paper — it communicates the\n829\t    entire equalizer paradox in one figure. The ρ=-0.94 correlation between aggregate\n830\t    convergence and per-example divergence quantifies the paradox and provides the\n831\t    anchor for the abstract''s headline result. Combined with the mechanism from exp-003\n832\t    and exp-004, the story is complete: attention creates aggregate convergence by\n833\t    compensating for architecture-specific blind spots, and the more attention you\n834\t    add, the more aggregate convergence AND the more per-example divergence you get.'\n835\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"9beb93de-aa17-4931-b36e-7a7ecdd76d11","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:17:01.442Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T135121/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Full equalizer paradox as stated. Three claims intact at original\n  strength. Dose-response across retrieval spectrum is the headline — LAMBADA is strongest\n  example, not the only one. Perplexity-based mechanism provides ''why'': attention\n  compensates for architecture-specific blind spots on high-uncertainty tokens. 4+\n  architectures preempts n=3 criticism. Target EMNLP Main with confidence.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (task-specificity validation)\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: 'Re-run lm-eval on 1.3B models with --log_samples for HellaSwag, ARC-Easy,\n    ARC-Challenge. Per-example convergence analysis replicating LAMBADA methodology\n    on each benchmark. 10×340M (RetNet+HGRN2) + 3×1.3B (GDN h3-1/h6-1/h24-1) runs\n    included.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    arc_easy_per_example_jaccard_mean: 0.08\n    arc_easy_std_reduction_pct: 42\n    arc_easy_spearman_rho: -0.7\n    arc_easy_spearman_p: 0.094\n    arc_easy_convergence_signal: clear but weaker than LAMBADA — per-example Jaccard\n      structure similar (low overlap, ~0.08), indicating architecture-specific effects\n      present\n    hellaswag_per_example_jaccard_mean: 0.15\n    hellaswag_std_reduction_pct: 8\n    hellaswag_spearman_rho: 0.1\n    hellaswag_spearman_p: 0.625\n    hellaswag_convergence_signal: confirmed non-converging at per-example level —\n      helped/hurt sets nearly random across attention ratios, no systematic pattern\n    arc_challenge_per_example_jaccard_mean: 0.12\n    arc_challenge_std_reduction_pct: 15\n    arc_challenge_spearman_rho: -0.3\n    arc_challenge_spearman_p: 0.347\n    arc_challenge_convergence_signal: weak, noisy — no systematic convergence structure\n    dose_response_rho_across_benchmarks: -0.89\n    n_benchmarks_significant_or_suggestive: 3\n    benchmarks_passing:\n    - LAMBADA (significant)\n    - ARC-Easy (suggestive, upgraded)\n    - MMLU (suggestive)\n  result_rationale: 'ARC-Easy upgrades from aggregate-only suggestive (ρ=-0.7) to\n    per-example-validated because its item format — single-hop factual recall from\n    encoded knowledge — shares the retrieval-demand property with LAMBADA. Per-example\n    analysis reveals low Jaccard (~0.08), confirming architecture-specific mechanisms\n    operate here too, just with smaller effect magnitude because ARC-Easy retrieval\n    is from parametric memory (less direct than LAMBADA''s in-context retrieval).\n    The per-example structure (low overlap, systematic helped/hurt sets) is the upgrade\n    signal, not a change in aggregate p-value. HellaSwag confirms non-convergence\n    at per-example level because its multi-step commonsense reasoning requires compositional\n    inference chains where attention doesn''t create a uniform architectural advantage\n    — each architecture''s recurrence mechanism handles reasoning chains idiosyncratically\n    but not in a way that converges with more attention. The helped/hurt sets are\n    near-random (Jaccard ~0.15, close to chance for 4-choice items), meaning attention\n    doesn''t systematically reorganize which samples each architecture handles. This\n    per-example confirmation is strictly stronger than \"aggregate ρ is non-significant\"\n    — it rules out hidden convergence masked by noise. The 3+ benchmark dose-response\n    gradient (LAMBADA ρ=-1.0, ARC-E ρ=-0.7 with structural validation, HellaSwag ρ≈0)\n    maps cleanly onto retrieval demand, yielding cross-benchmark dose-response ρ ≈\n    -0.89 when plotting convergence strength against retrieval demand index from published\n    task taxonomies.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table 2: Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B)'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Retrieval Demand Index\n    - Aggregate ρ\n    - Std Reduction %\n    - Mean Pairwise Jaccard\n    - Per-Example Convergence\n    - Classification\n    rows:\n    - - LAMBADA\n      - 0.95\n      - -1.0\n      - 79%\n      - 0.07\n      - Strong\n      - Converging\n    - - ARC-Easy\n      - 0.6\n      - -0.7\n      - 42%\n      - 0.08\n      - Moderate\n      - Suggestive (upgraded)\n    - - MMLU\n      - 0.45\n      - -0.7\n      - 35%\n      - 0.11\n      - Weak-moderate\n      - Suggestive\n    - - ARC-Challenge\n      - 0.35\n      - -0.3\n      - 15%\n      - 0.12\n      - Absent\n      - Non-converging\n    - - WinoGrande\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.1\n      - 8%\n      - 0.14\n      - Absent\n      - Non-converging\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.3\n      - 8%\n      - 0.15\n      - Absent (confirmed)\n      - Non-converging\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 3: Dose-response plot — x-axis: retrieval demand index (continuous, from\n    BIG-bench taxonomy), y-axis: convergence strength (Spearman ρ of cross-arch std\n    vs attention ratio). Six benchmarks as points with 95% bootstrap CIs. Fitted line\n    shows ρ=-0.89. LAMBADA and ARC-Easy cluster at high-retrieval/strong-convergence;\n    HellaSwag and WinoGrande at low-retrieval/no-convergence.'\n  - 'Figure 4: Per-example heatmaps for ARC-Easy (left) vs HellaSwag (right) — rows\n    = samples (sorted by aggregate effect), columns = architecture×attention_ratio.\n    ARC-Easy shows structured blocks (systematic helped/hurt), HellaSwag shows salt-and-pepper\n    noise (no structure).'\n  analysis: 'The per-example validation transforms the narrative from \"only LAMBADA\n    is significant\" to \"convergence strength is a continuous function of retrieval\n    demand, validated at per-example level.\" ARC-Easy''s structural similarity to\n    LAMBADA (low Jaccard, systematic helped/hurt sets) despite weaker aggregate effect\n    confirms the mechanism generalizes beyond a single benchmark. HellaSwag''s per-example\n    non-convergence rules out hidden effects masked by noise. The dose-response framing\n    (ρ=-0.89 across benchmarks) is the headline for Claim 1.\n\n    '\n- name: Helped/hurt sample characterization — Phase 2 (perplexity + topic/domain)\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: 'Phase 2 of exp-003. Compute baseline perplexity (pure recurrent model,\n    no attention) for each LAMBADA sample per architecture. Test whether helped/hurt\n    samples differ in baseline perplexity. Compute topic/domain distribution via simple\n    clustering on sentence embeddings. Cross-architecture comparison of perplexity\n    profiles.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    gdn_helped_mean_ppl: 245.3\n    gdn_hurt_mean_ppl: 89.7\n    gdn_ppl_mannwhitney_p: 0.0003\n    gdn_ppl_cohens_d: 0.58\n    hgrn2_helped_mean_ppl: 198.6\n    hgrn2_hurt_mean_ppl: 72.4\n    hgrn2_ppl_mannwhitney_p: 0.0008\n    hgrn2_ppl_cohens_d: 0.51\n    retnet_helped_mean_ppl: 267.1\n    retnet_hurt_mean_ppl: 95.3\n    retnet_ppl_mannwhitney_p: 0.0002\n    retnet_ppl_cohens_d: 0.62\n    cross_arch_ppl_ranking_correlation: 0.35\n    cross_arch_helped_overlap_given_high_ppl: 0.18\n    mechanism_summary: Attention rescues high-uncertainty tokens; which tokens are\n      high-uncertainty is architecture-specific\n    topic_cluster_effect: Marginal (p=0.04) — some domain skew but not primary driver\n  result_rationale: 'Baseline perplexity strongly differentiates helped vs hurt because\n    attention''s fundamental computational advantage is providing direct access to\n    earlier context positions — this matters most when the pure recurrent model is\n    uncertain (high perplexity = the recurrent state has lost or blurred the information\n    needed for prediction). Helped samples are those where the recurrent model''s\n    state is insufficient (high baseline ppl) and attention can \"rescue\" the prediction\n    by directly attending to the relevant context. Hurt samples are those the recurrent\n    model handles well (low baseline ppl) where attention introduces noise or distributes\n    probability mass suboptimally. The cross-architecture perplexity ranking correlation\n    is moderate (r≈0.35) rather than high because each recurrence mechanism (gated\n    delta rule, exponential decay, hierarchical gating) has different information\n    retention patterns — GDN''s delta rule loses different information than RetNet''s\n    exponential decay or HGRN2''s hierarchical gates. This explains both the universal\n    perplexity→effect direction (high ppl = helped, all architectures) AND the low\n    Jaccard overlap (different architectures are uncertain on different samples).\n    The mechanism is \"attention compensates for architecture-specific blind spots\n    on high-uncertainty tokens\" — elegant, testable, and explains both aggregate convergence\n    and per-example divergence simultaneously. Topic/domain shows marginal signal\n    because certain domains (e.g., technical vs narrative text) correlate with information\n    retention difficulty, but perplexity subsumes this — it''s the proximal cause.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table 4: Baseline perplexity differentiates helped vs hurt samples'\n    columns:\n    - Architecture\n    - Helped Mean PPL\n    - Hurt Mean PPL\n    - Mann-Whitney p\n    - Cohen's d\n    - Direction\n    rows:\n    - - GDN\n      - 245.3\n      - 89.7\n      - 0.0003\n      - 0.58\n      - Helped = high uncertainty\n    - - HGRN2\n      - 198.6\n      - 72.4\n      - 0.0008\n      - 0.51\n      - Helped = high uncertainty\n    - - RetNet\n      - 267.1\n      - 95.3\n      - 0.0002\n      - 0.62\n      - Helped = high uncertainty\n  - title: 'Table 5: Cross-architecture perplexity profile comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Arch Pair\n    - PPL Ranking ρ\n    - Helped Overlap (top-20% PPL)\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - GDN–HGRN2\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.2\n      - Moderate PPL correlation, low effect overlap\n    - - GDN–RetNet\n      - 0.32\n      - 0.16\n      - Low PPL correlation, very low effect overlap\n    - - HGRN2–RetNet\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.19\n      - Moderate PPL correlation, low effect overlap\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 5: Violin plots of baseline perplexity distributions for helped (green)\n    vs hurt (red) samples, one panel per architecture. Clear separation with helped\n    samples skewing high-perplexity in all three architectures.'\n  - 'Figure 6: Scatter plot — x-axis: GDN baseline perplexity per sample, y-axis:\n    RetNet baseline perplexity per sample. Points colored by helped/hurt status (GDN).\n    Shows moderate correlation in perplexity rankings but different helped/hurt assignments\n    — same underlying uncertainty landscape viewed through different architectural\n    lenses.'\n  analysis: 'Phase 2 cracks the mechanism. The universal direction (high ppl → helped)\n    combined with architecture-specific perplexity profiles provides a complete explanation\n    for the equalizer paradox: all architectures benefit from attention on their hardest\n    samples, but \"hardest\" is architecture-specific. This is the mechanistic core\n    of the paper — it transforms Claim 3 from \"non-overlapping effects exist (we don''t\n    know why)\" to \"non-overlapping effects exist BECAUSE each recurrence mechanism\n    has different information retention blind spots.\" The negative Phase 1 result\n    (surface features non-significant) strengthens this by ruling out simpler explanations.\n\n    '\n- name: Attention weight extraction and visualization\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'Extract attention weight patterns from h3-1 (3 attention layers out\n    of 24) hybrid models for GDN, RetNet, HGRN2 at 1.3B. Analyze attention distribution\n    over positions for helped vs hurt samples. Compute correlation between attention\n    entropy/concentration and helped/hurt identity.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    gdn_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.001\n    gdn_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect: 0.38\n    hgrn2_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.003\n    hgrn2_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect: 0.34\n    retnet_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p: 0.002\n    retnet_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect: 0.41\n    cross_arch_attention_pattern_similarity: 0.22\n    attention_focuses_on_target_antecedent: true\n    mean_correlation_across_archs: 0.38\n    n_archs_with_r_gt_0_3: 3\n  result_rationale: 'Attention layers in helped samples should show concentrated attention\n    patterns (low entropy) pointing to the context positions containing the information\n    needed for LAMBADA''s last-word prediction — specifically, the semantic antecedent\n    of the target word. For hurt samples, attention patterns should be more diffuse\n    (high entropy) because the recurrent state already encodes the necessary information,\n    and attention''s additional computation disperses probability mass without benefit.\n    The correlation r≈0.38 between attention concentration and helped/hurt identity\n    reflects that attention concentration is a strong but imperfect proxy — some concentrated-attention\n    samples are hurt because attention focuses on the wrong positions, and some diffuse-attention\n    samples are helped because even weak attention signal supplements a very weak\n    recurrent representation. Cross-architecture attention pattern similarity is low\n    (0.22) because each architecture''s recurrent state provides different partial\n    information, so attention must compensate for different gaps — GDN''s attention\n    might focus on positions 3-5 tokens back (compensating for delta-rule decay) while\n    RetNet''s focuses on positions 10-15 tokens back (compensating for exponential\n    decay''s longer-range loss). This directly visualizes the mechanism identified\n    in exp-003: attention rescues high-uncertainty predictions, and which positions\n    need rescuing is architecture-dependent.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table 6: Attention pattern metrics for helped vs hurt samples'\n    columns:\n    - Architecture\n    - Helped Attn Entropy\n    - Hurt Attn Entropy\n    - p-value\n    - Concentration–Effect r\n    rows:\n    - - GDN\n      - 2.31\n      - 3.14\n      - 0.001\n      - 0.38\n    - - HGRN2\n      - 2.48\n      - 3.22\n      - 0.003\n      - 0.34\n    - - RetNet\n      - 2.19\n      - 3.08\n      - 0.002\n      - 0.41\n  - title: 'Table 7: Cross-architecture attention focus position analysis'\n    columns:\n    - Architecture\n    - Modal Attention Distance (helped)\n    - Modal Attention Distance (hurt)\n    - Primary Compensation Target\n    rows:\n    - - GDN\n      - 3-7 tokens\n      - diffuse\n      - Recent context (delta-rule short-term decay)\n    - - RetNet\n      - 8-18 tokens\n      - diffuse\n      - Mid-range context (exponential decay)\n    - - HGRN2\n      - 4-12 tokens\n      - diffuse\n      - Variable (hierarchical gate-dependent)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 7: Attention heatmaps for 3 representative helped samples per architecture.\n    Each panel shows attention weights from the 3 attention layers. GDN attention\n    concentrates on recent positions; RetNet on mid-range; HGRN2 variable. Demonstrates\n    architecture-specific compensation targets.'\n  - 'Figure 8: Scatter plot — x-axis: attention entropy at last-token position, y-axis:\n    per-example effect magnitude. Three colors for three architectures. All show negative\n    correlation (concentrated attention → larger positive effect). Closes the mechanistic\n    loop.'\n  analysis: 'Attention weight analysis closes the mechanistic story. The three-part\n    causal chain is now: (1) each recurrence mechanism has different information retention\n    blind spots (exp-003: architecture-specific perplexity profiles), (2) attention\n    layers compensate by focusing on the positions where information was lost (exp-004:\n    concentrated attention on architecture-specific positions), (3) this produces\n    aggregate convergence through non-overlapping per-example effects (exp-001/002:\n    equalizer paradox). The r>0.3 threshold is met for all three architectures, with\n    mean r=0.38. Combined with exp-003''s perplexity mechanism, this provides sufficient\n    mechanistic depth for Reviewer Concern C.\n\n    '\n- name: Architecture expansion check\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: 'Search Wang et al. model zoo for additional architectures beyond GDN,\n    RetNet, HGRN2. Evaluate Mamba2-hybrid (if available) or other hybrid architecture\n    on LAMBADA with --log_samples at 1.3B scale. Test convergence pattern.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    additional_arch_found: Mamba2-hybrid (1.3B, attention ratios h0/h3-1/h6-1)\n    mamba2_h0_lambada_acc: 0.612\n    mamba2_h3_1_lambada_acc: 0.648\n    mamba2_h6_1_lambada_acc: 0.659\n    cross_arch_std_h0: 0.031\n    cross_arch_std_h3_1: 0.012\n    cross_arch_std_h6_1: 0.008\n    convergence_confirmed: true\n    mamba2_jaccard_vs_gdn: 0.06\n    mamba2_jaccard_vs_retnet: 0.07\n    mamba2_jaccard_vs_hgrn2: 0.09\n    n_architectures_tested: 4\n    updated_spearman_rho: -1.0\n    updated_spearman_p: 0.003\n  result_rationale: 'Wang et al.''s model zoo includes multiple architecture families.\n    Mamba2-hybrid is the most likely available addition because Mamba/Mamba2 is a\n    prominent linear recurrent architecture and hybrid variants are a natural extension\n    of the zoo. Mamba2''s selective state-space mechanism is fundamentally different\n    from GDN''s delta rule, RetNet''s exponential decay, and HGRN2''s hierarchical\n    gating — so it represents a genuinely independent test of the convergence hypothesis.\n    With a 4th architecture, the convergence pattern strengthens: adding Mamba2 to\n    the cross-arch std computation increases n from 3 to 4 at each attention ratio,\n    making the monotonic decrease more robust. The updated Spearman ρ remains -1.0\n    but with improved p-value (0.003 vs 0.017 with n=3) because n=4 provides more\n    permutation samples. Mamba2''s per-example Jaccard against existing architectures\n    is very low (0.06-0.09) consistent with the equalizer paradox — its selective\n    SSM mechanism has yet another distinct set of blind spots that attention compensates\n    for. This is the single most impactful experiment for reviewer confidence: going\n    from n=3 to n=4 architectures preempts the \"too few architectures\" criticism (Concern\n    D) and makes the convergence finding look systematic rather than coincidental.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table 8: Cross-architecture std with 4 architectures (1.3B LAMBADA)'\n    columns:\n    - Attention Ratio\n    - N Archs\n    - Cross-Arch Std\n    - Std (3-arch, original)\n    rows:\n    - - h0 (pure)\n      - 4\n      - 0.031\n      - 0.024\n    - - h3-1\n      - 4\n      - 0.012\n      - 0.01\n    - - h6-1\n      - 4\n      - 0.008\n      - 0.007\n    - - h12-1\n      - 4\n      - 0.006\n      - 0.005\n    - - h24-1\n      - 4\n      - 0.005\n      - 0.005\n  - title: 'Table 9: Pairwise Jaccard matrix (4 architectures, helped/hurt sets)'\n    columns:\n    - ''\n    - GDN\n    - RetNet\n    - HGRN2\n    - Mamba2\n    rows:\n    - - GDN\n      - —\n      - 0.05\n      - 0.07\n      - 0.06\n    - - RetNet\n      - 0.05\n      - —\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.07\n    - - HGRN2\n      - 0.07\n      - 0.08\n      - —\n      - 0.09\n    - - Mamba2\n      - 0.06\n      - 0.07\n      - 0.09\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 2 (updated): Cross-architecture std vs attention ratio with 4 architectures.\n    Monotonic decrease confirmed. Shaded region shows 95% bootstrap CI. Mamba2 addition\n    widens the initial spread (h0) but convergence to near-identical performance at\n    h24-1 is even more striking.'\n  analysis: 'Adding Mamba2 as a 4th architecture is the strongest single upgrade to\n    the paper. It moves the finding from \"3 architectures happen to converge\" to \"4\n    fundamentally different recurrence mechanisms all converge under attention\" —\n    a much stronger inductive base. The per-example Jaccard values confirm Mamba2\n    also has architecture- specific effects, extending the equalizer paradox to a\n    4th mechanism. Reviewers asking \"is n=3 enough?\" now face n=4 spanning delta rule,\n    exponential decay, hierarchical gating, and selective state spaces.\n\n    '\n- name: Statistical robustness framework\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'Bootstrap CIs for all convergence metrics. GDN-excluded sensitivity\n    analysis (RetNet + HGRN2 pairwise). Multi-comparison correction. Effect sizes.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    lambada_std_reduction_bootstrap_ci_95:\n    - 0.65\n    - 0.88\n    lambada_std_reduction_bootstrap_ci_lower: 0.65\n    lambada_gdn_excluded_std_reduction_pct: 62\n    lambada_gdn_excluded_spearman_rho: -1.0\n    lambada_gdn_excluded_bootstrap_ci_95:\n    - 0.42\n    - 0.78\n    lambada_gdn_excluded_bootstrap_ci_lower: 0.42\n    lambada_gdn_excluded_pairwise_monotonic: true\n    cohens_d_h0_vs_h24: 1.82\n    eta_squared_attention_ratio: 0.74\n    bonferroni_corrected_lambada_p: 0.048\n    fdr_corrected_lambada_p: 0.024\n    fdr_corrected_arc_easy_p: 0.234\n    all_claims_survive_gdn_exclusion: true\n    per_example_bootstrap_jaccard_ci_95:\n    - 0.04\n    - 0.11\n  result_rationale: 'Bootstrap CIs for std reduction are tight ([0.65, 0.88]) because\n    the convergence signal on LAMBADA is strong and consistent across all 5 attention\n    ratios — there''s little sampling variance in the monotonic decrease pattern.\n    The lower bound (0.65) comfortably exceeds zero, confirming the effect is real.\n    GDN-excluded analysis (RetNet + HGRN2 only) still shows monotonic convergence\n    because the equalizer effect is not an artifact of GDN''s D004 training confound\n    — RetNet and HGRN2 independently converge toward each other as attention increases.\n    The pairwise std reduction is 62% (lower than 79% with all three because GDN happened\n    to be the most extreme outlier at h0, amplifying the convergence magnitude). The\n    GDN- excluded bootstrap CI lower bound (0.42) is above zero but lower than the\n    full analysis, correctly reflecting reduced statistical power with n=2 architectures\n    instead of n=3. With n=4 (post-exp-005 Mamba2 addition), GDN-excluded analysis\n    uses n=3 remaining architectures, yielding a stronger CI [0.55, 0.82] with lower\n    bound 0.55 comfortably in the target range. Bonferroni correction across 6 benchmarks\n    pushes LAMBADA''s p from 0.008 to 0.048 — still significant at α=0.05. FDR correction\n    (Benjamini-Hochberg) is less conservative at p=0.024. The large Cohen''s d (1.82)\n    and η² (0.74) confirm this is a large effect, not a statistically significant\n    triviality.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table 3: Bootstrap confidence intervals for convergence metrics'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Point Estimate\n    - Bootstrap 95% CI\n    - N (bootstrap samples)\n    rows:\n    - - Std reduction (3 arch)\n      - 79%\n      - '[0.65, 0.88]'\n      - 10000\n    - - Std reduction (GDN-excluded, 2 arch)\n      - 62%\n      - '[0.42, 0.78]'\n      - 10000\n    - - Std reduction (GDN-excluded, 3 arch post-exp-005)\n      - 68%\n      - '[0.55, 0.82]'\n      - 10000\n    - - Spearman ρ (LAMBADA)\n      - -1.0\n      - '[-1.0, -0.90]'\n      - 10000\n    - - Per-example Jaccard (mean pairwise)\n      - 0.07\n      - '[0.04, 0.11]'\n      - 10000\n  - title: 'Table 3b: Multi-comparison corrected p-values'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Raw p\n    - Bonferroni p\n    - FDR p\n    - Significant (α=0.05)\n    rows:\n    - - LAMBADA\n      - 0.008\n      - 0.048\n      - 0.024\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - ARC-Easy\n      - 0.117\n      - 0.702\n      - 0.234\n      - 'No'\n    - - MMLU\n      - 0.117\n      - 0.702\n      - 0.234\n      - 'No'\n    - - ARC-Challenge\n      - 0.347\n      - 1.0\n      - 0.416\n      - 'No'\n    - - WinoGrande\n      - 0.608\n      - 1.0\n      - 0.608\n      - 'No'\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - 0.742\n      - 1.0\n      - 0.742\n      - 'No'\n  - title: 'Table 3c: Effect sizes'\n    columns:\n    - Comparison\n    - Cohen's d\n    - η²\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - h0 vs h24 cross-arch std\n      - 1.82\n      - —\n      - Very large\n    - - Attention ratio → std (ANOVA)\n      - —\n      - 0.74\n      - Very large\n    - - Helped vs hurt PPL difference\n      - 0.55\n      - —\n      - Medium-large\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S1 (Supplementary): Bootstrap distribution of std reduction percentage.\n    Histogram with 95% CI shaded. Full (3-arch) and GDN-excluded overlaid. Both distributions\n    clearly above zero.'\n  analysis: 'The robustness framework provides the statistical armor the paper needs.\n    Key results: (1) LAMBADA convergence survives Bonferroni at α=0.05, (2) GDN exclusion\n    reduces magnitude but doesn''t eliminate the effect — the D004 confound is not\n    driving the finding, (3) effect sizes are large (d>1.5, η²>0.7), placing this\n    well above \"statistically significant but trivial\" territory. The bootstrap CIs\n    are the cleanest defense against Reviewer skepticism — the lower bound never crosses\n    zero.\n\n    '\n- name: 340M rebound mechanism analysis\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'Deep per-example analysis of 340M non-monotonic rebound at high attention\n    ratios. Cross-scale comparison with 1.3B at matched attention ratios. Characterize\n    which samples diverge between scales.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rebound_samples_count: 127\n    rebound_samples_mean_baseline_ppl: 412.5\n    rebound_samples_pct_in_top_quartile_difficulty: 0.78\n    matched_1_3b_accuracy_on_rebound_samples: 0.71\n    matched_340m_accuracy_on_rebound_samples: 0.34\n    capacity_bottleneck_confirmed: true\n    rebound_onset_attention_ratio: h12-1 (50% attention)\n    rebound_magnitude_std_increase: 0.008\n    training_tokens_confound_assessment: Partially contributing but not primary —\n      340M at matched training tokens still shows rebound pattern\n    scale_mechanism: At 340M, model capacity is insufficient to simultaneously maintain\n      recurrent state quality AND leverage attention effectively on the hardest samples.\n      At h12+ attention ratios, attention layers 'crowd out' recurrent capacity, degrading\n      performance on samples that the pure recurrent model marginally handled.\n  result_rationale: 'The 340M rebound at high attention ratios occurs because model\n    capacity is finite and attention has overhead costs. At 1.3B, there''s sufficient\n    capacity to both maintain high-quality recurrent representations AND run effective\n    attention — the two mechanisms complement each other. At 340M, adding more attention\n    layers beyond a threshold (around h12-1) starts to degrade the recurrent pathway''s\n    quality because parameters are reallocated from recurrence to attention. The \"rebound\n    samples\" — those that 340M gets right at intermediate attention but wrong at high\n    attention — are overwhelmingly the hardest samples (top quartile of baseline perplexity).\n    These are samples where the recurrent state barely encodes enough information,\n    and when recurrent capacity is reduced (by shifting parameters to attention),\n    they fall below the threshold. At 1.3B, the same samples are handled because there''s\n    enough total capacity for both pathways. This capacity-bottleneck explanation\n    turns the 340M rebound from an inconvenient non-monotonicity into positive evidence\n    for the equalizer theory: convergence requires sufficient capacity, and the failure\n    mode at 340M (rebound on hardest samples) is exactly what the mechanism predicts.\n    The training-tokens confound (340M trained on 20B tokens vs 1.3B on 100B) is partially\n    contributing — less training means worse recurrent representations overall — but\n    the rebound pattern specifically (non-monotonic at high attention) persists even\n    when accounting for this, because it''s driven by parameter allocation rather\n    than training duration.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table 10: 340M rebound sample characterization'\n    columns:\n    - Sample Subset\n    - N\n    - Mean Baseline PPL\n    - 340M h6-1 Acc\n    - 340M h24-1 Acc\n    - 1.3B h24-1 Acc\n    rows:\n    - - Rebound (340M regresses at high attn)\n      - 127\n      - 412.5\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.71\n    - - Monotonic-improving (340M matches 1.3B)\n      - 389\n      - 156.3\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.62\n    - - Stable-correct (always right)\n      - 1847\n      - 45.2\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.91\n      - 0.95\n    - - Stable-wrong (always wrong)\n      - 698\n      - 523.7\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.06\n      - 0.31\n  - title: 'Table 11: Scale comparison — convergence completeness'\n    columns:\n    - Scale\n    - Monotonic?\n    - Std Reduction %\n    - Rebound Onset\n    - Mechanism\n    rows:\n    - - 1.3B\n      - 'Yes'\n      - 79%\n      - None\n      - Sufficient capacity for both pathways\n    - - 340M\n      - No (rebound at h12+)\n      - 51% (peak at h6-1)\n      - h12-1\n      - Capacity bottleneck on hardest samples\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 9: Cross-arch std vs attention ratio at both scales. 1.3B shows monotonic\n    decrease. 340M shows decrease then rebound. Vertical dashed line at h12-1 marks\n    rebound onset. Annotated with sample counts in each regime.'\n  - 'Figure 10: Difficulty distribution of rebound samples vs non-rebound samples.\n    Clear separation — rebound samples cluster at high baseline perplexity (hardest\n    quartile).'\n  analysis: 'The 340M rebound mechanism analysis is the linchpin of Claim 2 in the\n    absence of 7B data. It provides a mechanistic explanation for WHY scale matters:\n    the equalizer effect requires sufficient model capacity to run both recurrent\n    and attention pathways effectively. At 340M, this capacity constraint creates\n    a non-monotonic pattern specifically on the hardest samples — exactly the samples\n    where the equalizer mechanism (attention rescuing high-uncertainty predictions)\n    needs the most capacity. This transforms Claim 2 from \"scale matters (we have\n    two points)\" to \"scale determines whether capacity is sufficient for the equalizer\n    mechanism, with a predictable failure mode when it isn''t.\" The 78% of rebound\n    samples being in the top difficulty quartile is strong evidence for the capacity-bottleneck\n    interpretation.\n\n    '\n- name: Convergence-divergence bridge analysis\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: 'Plot aggregate convergence metrics alongside per-example divergence\n    metrics as function of attention ratio for LAMBADA. Show co-evolution of convergence\n    and divergence. Compute correlation between convergence and divergence trajectories.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    convergence_divergence_correlation_rho: -0.94\n    convergence_divergence_correlation_p: 0.005\n    max_divergence_attention_ratio: h24-1\n    per_example_effect_magnitude_slope: positive (more attention → larger per-example\n      effects)\n    net_effect_asymmetry_gdn:\n    - -0.15\n    - -0.12\n    - -0.09\n    - -0.11\n    - -0.14\n    net_effect_asymmetry_hgrn2:\n    - 0.08\n    - 0.11\n    - 0.14\n    - 0.16\n    - 0.15\n    jaccard_trend:\n    - 0.09\n    - 0.07\n    - 0.06\n    - 0.05\n    - 0.05\n    paradox_quantified: true\n  result_rationale: 'The convergence-divergence co-evolution is the most visually\n    striking result in the paper because it directly demonstrates the paradox. As\n    attention ratio increases: (1) aggregate cross-arch std monotonically decreases\n    (convergence), (2) per-example effect magnitudes increase (each architecture''s\n    helped/hurt effects get stronger), (3) pairwise Jaccard decreases (the helped/hurt\n    sets become MORE architecture-specific, not less). The correlation between convergence\n    and divergence is strongly negative (ρ=-0.94) because they are mechanistically\n    linked: more attention means more compensation for architecture-specific blind\n    spots, which means both more aggregate convergence (the blind spots are compensated)\n    AND more per-example divergence (the compensation is architecture-specific). This\n    is not a contradiction — it''s the same mechanism viewed from two levels of analysis.\n    The net effect asymmetry (GDN hurt, HGRN2 helped) is stable across attention ratios\n    because it reflects the relative quality of each architecture''s baseline recurrent\n    representations on LAMBADA: HGRN2''s hierarchical gating is better suited to LAMBADA''s\n    retrieval structure, so adding attention helps HGRN2 proportionally less on easy\n    samples but more on hard ones, yielding net positive; GDN''s delta rule is less\n    suited, so attention''s noise on easy samples outweighs rescue on hard ones, yielding\n    net negative.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table 12: Convergence-divergence co-evolution across attention ratios'\n    columns:\n    - Attention Ratio\n    - Cross-Arch Std\n    - Mean |Effect|\n    - Mean Pairwise Jaccard\n    - GDN Net\n    - HGRN2 Net\n    rows:\n    - - h0\n      - 0.024\n      - 0.0\n      - —\n      - 0.0\n      - 0.0\n    - - h3-1\n      - 0.018\n      - 0.032\n      - 0.09\n      - -0.15\n      - 0.08\n    - - h6-1\n      - 0.012\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.07\n      - -0.12\n      - 0.11\n    - - h12-1\n      - 0.007\n      - 0.048\n      - 0.06\n      - -0.09\n      - 0.14\n    - - h24-1\n      - 0.005\n      - 0.055\n      - 0.05\n      - -0.14\n      - 0.15\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 1 (paper headline figure): Dual-axis plot. Left y-axis: cross-architecture\n    std (blue, decreasing). Right y-axis: mean per-example effect magnitude (red,\n    increasing) and mean pairwise Jaccard (orange, decreasing). x-axis: attention\n    ratio. The crossing/diverging trajectories visually encode the paradox. This is\n    the figure that makes the paper.'\n  - 'Figure 11: Stacked bar chart showing net effect direction (helped vs hurt balance)\n    per architecture at each attention ratio. GDN bars below zero (net hurt), HGRN2\n    bars above (net helped), RetNet near zero. Shows symmetric but opposite effects\n    that produce aggregate convergence.'\n  analysis: 'This is the synthesis experiment that ties everything together into the\n    headline figure. The dual-axis plot showing convergence and divergence co-evolving\n    is the single most important visualization in the paper — it communicates the\n    entire equalizer paradox in one figure. The ρ=-0.94 correlation between aggregate\n    convergence and per-example divergence quantifies the paradox and provides the\n    anchor for the abstract''s headline result. Combined with the mechanism from exp-003\n    and exp-004, the story is complete: attention creates aggregate convergence by\n    compensating for architecture-specific blind spots, and the more attention you\n    add, the more aggregate convergence AND the more per-example divergence you get.'\n","numLines":835,"startLine":1,"totalLines":835}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013kD4MdwG93c37ai5iMWwk2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read the full PDF (9 pages) and the review context YAML. Let me now dispatch two parallel reviewers:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nThen I'll run the full roundtable (Steps 1-4) and submit the report.\n\nLet me carefully analyze the paper first, then dispatch the two reviewers as parallel agents.\n\nLet me think about the paper's content:\n\n**Paper Summary**: \"The Equalizer Paradox\" — studies hybrid linear recurrent architectures (GDN, RetNet, HGRN2, Mamba2) with varying attention ratios. \n\nThe central finding is that attention reduces variance across architectures at the aggregate level while producing non-overlapping per-example effects. The paper makes three main claims around dose-response relationships with retrieval demand, scale-dependent convergence patterns, and a perplexity-based mechanism. I'm noticing the paper is 8 pages of main text plus tables, which fits within limits, but all references are broken with [?] or [??] markers — a significant formatting problem. The PDF also references 11 figures throughout but none are actually rendered.\n\nThe experiments use pretrained models from Wang et al. (2025) across 4 architectures, 5 attention ratios, and 6 benchmarks. Statistical rigor is solid with bootstrap confidence intervals, multiple comparison corrections, and effect sizes, though only LAMBADA survives the correction. I can see Section 6 covers limitations, but there's no visible ethics checklist. Checking against the experiment plan, the multi-benchmark per-example analysis appears in Section 4.1 with Table 1, and the helped/hurt perplexity breakdown is in Section 4.5 with Tables 8.\n\nAll seven planned experiments are accounted for in the paper — attention weight analysis in Section 4.6, Mamba2 expansion in Section 4.3, statistical robustness in Section 4.2, the 340M rebound effect in Section 4.7, and the convergence-divergence bridge in Section 4.4. Now I'll set up the parallel reviewer dispatch to get their assessments.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34954,"cache_read_input_tokens":23646,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34954},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"eb4892f7-40b7-4a73-abdf-5985580f0f67"}
