{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKhTwQvJBV9QakW75DKtix","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n14\t\\usepackage{array}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcolumntype{C}{>{\\centering\\arraybackslash}X}\n17\t\\newcolumntype{L}{>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{The Equalizer Paradox: Attention Induces Aggregate Convergence\\\\Through Per-Example Divergence in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\\date{}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t% ============================================================\n28\t% ABSTRACT\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t\\begin{abstract}\n31\tHybrid architectures that interleave linear recurrence with full attention are rapidly becoming the dominant paradigm for efficient language models, yet how attention reshapes behavior \\emph{across} different recurrence mechanisms remains poorly understood.\n32\tWe present a systematic per-example analysis of 4 hybrid linear recurrent architectures---GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2, and Mamba2---spanning 5 attention ratios at 340M and 1.3B parameters.\n33\tOur central finding is the \\textbf{equalizer paradox}: adding attention reduces cross-architecture performance variance by up to 79\\% on retrieval-intensive tasks (LAMBADA cross-architecture std 0.024$\\to$0.005, Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}1.0$, $p{=}0.003$), yet the per-example effects are almost entirely non-overlapping across architectures (pairwise Jaccard $<$\\,0.10).\n34\tWe establish three claims: (1) convergence strength is a continuous function of task retrieval demand ($\\rho{=}{-}0.89$ across 6 benchmarks), with LAMBADA as the strongest case; (2) convergence is scale-dependent, monotonically complete at 1.3B but exhibiting a capacity-bottleneck rebound at 340M; and (3) the mechanism is perplexity-based compensation---attention rescues high-uncertainty tokens whose identity is architecture-specific (helped vs.\\ hurt baseline perplexity: Cohen's $d$\\,$\\in$\\,[0.51,\\,0.62], all $p<0.001$).\n35\tThese results imply that aggregate benchmark parity masks fundamental differences in how hybrid architectures process language, with direct consequences for architecture selection.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t% ============================================================\n39\t% 1  INTRODUCTION\n40\t% ============================================================\n41\t\\section{Introduction}\n42\t\n43\tLinear recurrent architectures---including gated state-space models, linear attention variants, and hierarchical gated RNNs---have emerged as efficient alternatives to Transformers, offering linear-time inference while approaching Transformer-level quality \\citep{wang2025mamba}.\n44\tA dominant design pattern \\emph{hybridizes} these recurrent layers with a fraction of full-attention layers, creating models that combine the efficiency of recurrence with the expressiveness of attention.\n45\tWang et al.\\ (2025) provide the most comprehensive model zoo to date, training 72 hybrid models across three recurrence mechanisms, multiple attention ratios, and two scales.\n46\tThey observe that aggregate performance metrics tend to converge as the attention fraction increases, but they do not analyze the mechanism behind this convergence.\n47\t\n48\tWe present the first quantitative characterization of what we term the \\textbf{equalizer effect}: the systematic convergence of aggregate cross-architecture performance as attention increases.\n49\tOur per-example analysis reveals that this convergence conceals a paradox.\n50\tAt the sample level, attention affects almost entirely \\emph{different} subsets of examples for each architecture (pairwise Jaccard overlap $<$\\,0.10), with architectures being helped and hurt on different items.\n51\tAggregate convergence emerges not because architectures become functionally equivalent, but because attention compensates for architecture-specific blind spots---and the complementarity of these compensations averages out at the benchmark level.\n52\t\n53\tWe organize our findings around three claims:\n54\t\n55\t\\textbf{Claim 1: Dose-response convergence across the retrieval spectrum.}\n56\tThe strength of the equalizer effect scales continuously with task retrieval demand.\n57\tOn LAMBADA (high retrieval demand), cross-architecture standard deviation drops 79\\% as attention increases ($\\rho{=}{-}1.0$, $p{=}0.003$ with 4 architectures).\n58\tOn ARC-Easy (moderate retrieval demand), convergence is suggestive ($\\rho{=}{-}0.7$).\n59\tOn reasoning-heavy tasks (HellaSwag, WinoGrande), convergence is absent.\n60\tThe cross-benchmark dose-response gradient is $\\rho{=}{-}0.89$.\n61\t\n62\t\\textbf{Claim 2: Scale-dependent convergence.}\n63\tAt 1.3B parameters, convergence is monotonically complete.\n64\tAt 340M, a rebound appears at high attention ratios ($\\geq$\\,50\\%), driven by a capacity bottleneck: 78\\% of rebounding samples fall in the top difficulty quartile.\n65\tThis establishes that sufficient model capacity is a necessary condition for the equalizer mechanism.\n66\t\n67\t\\textbf{Claim 3: Perplexity-based mechanistic explanation.}\n68\tAttention rescues tokens that are high-uncertainty under the pure recurrent model.\n69\tWhich tokens are high-uncertainty is architecture-specific (cross-architecture perplexity ranking $\\rho \\approx 0.35$, helped-set overlap $\\approx$\\,18\\%).\n70\tThis explains both the aggregate convergence (all architectures benefit on their hardest items) and the per-example divergence (each architecture's hardest items are different).\n71\t\n72\tTogether, these findings reveal that hybrid architectures achieving similar aggregate scores may be processing language in fundamentally different ways---a result with direct implications for architecture selection, ensembling, and the interpretation of benchmark comparisons.\n73\t\n74\t\n75\t% ============================================================\n76\t% 2  RELATED WORK\n77\t% ============================================================\n78\t\\section{Related Work}\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{Linear recurrent architectures.}\n81\tThe landscape of efficient sequence models has diversified rapidly.\n82\tRetNet \\citep{sun2023retentive} uses multi-scale exponential decay for linear-complexity retention.\n83\tHGRN2 \\citep{qin2024hgrn2} introduces hierarchical gating for state management.\n84\tGatedDeltaNet (GDN) applies a gated delta rule to state updates.\n85\tMamba and Mamba2 \\citep{gu2023mamba,dao2024mamba2} use selective state-space mechanisms with input-dependent gating.\n86\tEach mechanism encodes context differently, creating the preconditions for architecture-specific behavior.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Hybrid architectures.}\n89\tCombining recurrent and attention layers has become standard practice.\n90\tWang et al.\\ (2025) systematically vary the attention ratio across architectures and scales, demonstrating that even a small fraction of attention layers substantially improves downstream performance.\n91\tTheir model zoo (72 models across 3 architectures, 5 attention ratios, 2 scales) provides the empirical basis for our analysis.\n92\tWhile they note aggregate convergence trends, they do not perform per-example analysis or investigate the mechanism.\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{Per-example analysis in NLP.}\n95\tInstance-level analysis has yielded insights in machine translation \\citep{vilar2006error}, dataset cartography \\citep{swayamdipta2020dataset}, and model comparison.\n96\tOur use of per-example helped/hurt sets and Jaccard overlap draws on the tradition of item response theory applied to NLP benchmarks.\n97\t\n98\t% ============================================================\n99\t% 3  EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n100\t% ============================================================\n101\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Models}\n104\t\n105\tWe use models from the Wang et al.\\ (2025) model zoo, spanning four recurrence mechanisms:\n106\t\\begin{itemize}\n107\t\\item \\textbf{GatedDeltaNet (GDN)}: Gated delta rule for state updates.\n108\t\\item \\textbf{RetNet}: Multi-scale exponential decay (linear attention variant).\n109\t\\item \\textbf{HGRN2}: Hierarchical gated recurrence with outer-product state expansion.\n110\t\\item \\textbf{Mamba2}: Selective state-space model with input-dependent gating.\n111\t\\end{itemize}\n112\t\n113\tFor each architecture, we evaluate models at 1.3B parameters with five attention ratios: h0 (pure recurrent), h3-1 (3 of 24 layers are attention $\\approx$\\,12.5\\%), h6-1 (25\\%), h12-1 (50\\%), and h24-1 (100\\% attention).\n114\tFor GDN, RetNet, and HGRN2, we additionally evaluate at 340M parameters.\n115\t\n116\t\\subsection{Benchmarks}\n117\t\n118\tWe evaluate on six benchmarks spanning a range of retrieval demand:\n119\tLAMBADA (last-word prediction, high retrieval demand),\n120\tARC-Easy and ARC-Challenge (science QA),\n121\tMMLU (multi-domain knowledge),\n122\tWinoGrande (coreference resolution),\n123\tand HellaSwag (commonsense reasoning, low retrieval demand).\n124\tAll evaluations use \\texttt{lm-evaluation-harness} with \\texttt{--log\\_samples} to obtain per-example predictions.\n125\t\n126\t\\subsection{Analysis Framework}\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{Aggregate convergence.}\n129\tFor each benchmark and attention ratio, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation of accuracy.\n130\tConvergence is measured as the Spearman correlation between attention ratio and cross-architecture std.\n131\t\n132\t\\paragraph{Per-example analysis.}\n133\tFor each architecture pair at a given attention ratio, we define the \\emph{helped set} (samples correct with attention but incorrect without) and the \\emph{hurt set} (correct without but incorrect with).\n134\tWe compute pairwise Jaccard similarity between helped/hurt sets across architectures.\n135\t\n136\t\\paragraph{Convergence--divergence bridge.}\n137\tWe jointly track aggregate convergence (cross-architecture std) and per-example divergence (effect magnitude and Jaccard) as a function of attention ratio to quantify the equalizer paradox.\n138\t\n139\t\n140\t% ============================================================\n141\t% 4  RESULTS\n142\t% ============================================================\n143\t\\section{Results}\n144\t\n145\t\\subsection{Dose-Response Convergence Across the Retrieval Spectrum}\n146\t\\label{sec:dose-response}\n147\t\n148\tTable~\\ref{tab:convergence-benchmarks} presents per-example convergence metrics across six benchmarks at 1.3B scale.\n149\tThe convergence strength varies continuously with retrieval demand rather than following a binary retrieval/reasoning split.\n150\t\n151\tLAMBADA exhibits the strongest convergence signal: aggregate Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}1.0$ between attention ratio and cross-architecture std, with 79\\% std reduction and strong per-example convergence (mean pairwise Jaccard = 0.07).\n152\tARC-Easy shows moderate convergence ($\\rho{=}{-}0.7$, 42\\% std reduction) with per-example structure similar to LAMBADA (Jaccard $\\approx$\\,0.08, low overlap confirming architecture-specific effects).\n153\tMMLU exhibits weak-moderate convergence ($\\rho{=}{-}0.7$, 35\\% std reduction).\n154\tBenchmarks with low retrieval demand---ARC-Challenge, WinoGrande, HellaSwag---show no systematic convergence.\n155\t\n156\tPlotting convergence strength against a retrieval demand index derived from published task taxonomies yields a cross-benchmark dose-response correlation of $\\rho{=}{-}0.89$.\n157\tThis continuous relationship is the headline finding of Claim~1: the equalizer effect is not specific to LAMBADA but modulated by retrieval demand, with LAMBADA as the strongest exemplar.\n158\t\n159\tHellaSwag's per-example analysis is particularly informative.\n160\tAt the aggregate level, the non-significant $\\rho$ could reflect either genuine absence of convergence or convergence masked by noise.\n161\tThe per-example analysis (Jaccard = 0.15, near chance for 4-choice items; helped/hurt sets nearly random across architectures) confirms genuine non-convergence, ruling out hidden effects.\n162\t\n163\t\\begin{table}[t]\n164\t\\centering\n165\t\\small\n166\t\\caption{Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B scale). Retrieval Demand Index derived from published task taxonomies. Per-example convergence assessed via Jaccard overlap and systematic helped/hurt structure.}\n167\t\\label{tab:convergence-benchmarks}\n168\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n169\t\\toprule\n170\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Retr.\\ Dem.} & \\textbf{Agg.\\ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Std Red.\\ \\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard} & \\textbf{Per-Ex.\\ Conv.} & \\textbf{Class} \\\\\n171\t\\midrule\n172\tLAMBADA       & 0.95 & $-$1.0 & 79\\% & 0.07 & Strong   & Converging \\\\\n173\tARC-Easy      & 0.60 & $-$0.7 & 42\\% & 0.08 & Moderate & Suggestive \\\\\n174\tMMLU          & 0.45 & $-$0.7 & 35\\% & 0.11 & Weak-mod.  & Suggestive \\\\\n175\tARC-Chall.    & 0.35 & $-$0.3 & 15\\% & 0.12 & Absent   & Non-conv. \\\\\n176\tWinoGrande    & 0.30 & 0.1  & 8\\%  & 0.14 & Absent   & Non-conv. \\\\\n177\tHellaSwag     & 0.15 & 0.3  & 8\\%  & 0.15 & Absent   & Non-conv. \\\\\n178\t\\bottomrule\n179\t\\end{tabular}\n180\t\\end{table}\n181\t\n182\t% -------------------------------------------------------\n183\t\\subsection{Statistical Robustness}\n184\t\\label{sec:robustness}\n185\t\n186\tWe apply a comprehensive statistical robustness framework to ensure the convergence findings are not artifacts of small samples or multiple comparisons.\n187\t\n188\t\\paragraph{Bootstrap confidence intervals.}\n189\tTable~\\ref{tab:bootstrap} reports bootstrap CIs (10{,}000 resamples) for the primary convergence metrics.\n190\tThe 95\\% CI for LAMBADA std reduction is [0.65,\\,0.88], with the lower bound comfortably above zero.\n191\tEven under GDN-excluded sensitivity analysis (testing whether GDN's training confound drives the result), the effect persists: 62\\% std reduction with CI [0.42,\\,0.78] (2 architectures) and 68\\% with CI [0.55,\\,0.82] after adding Mamba2 (3 architectures, GDN-excluded).\n192\t\n193\t\\begin{table}[t]\n194\t\\centering\n195\t\\small\n196\t\\caption{Bootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals for convergence metrics (10{,}000 resamples).}\n197\t\\label{tab:bootstrap}\n198\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n199\t\\toprule\n200\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Point Est.} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & \\textbf{N} \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"93ac4469-fdf2-4a77-9df8-9b10992651b1","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:18:20.233Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SaT85sgYBwibPVjXjjkk3v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KJ8UFQi4JHAFnyGp1rqezg","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T131014/best/paper.tex","limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1513,"cache_read_input_tokens":17983,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1513,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":70,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"3c8da79b-4429-4390-99b0-51e38ad74f7e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KJ8UFQi4JHAFnyGp1rqezg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n11\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n12\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n13\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n14\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n15\t\\usepackage{url}\n16\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{The Equalizer Paradox: Attention Induces Aggregate Convergence Through Architecture-Specific Mechanisms in Hybrid Recurrent Models}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{Anonymous}\n21\t\n22\t\\date{}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\tHybrid architectures that interleave linear recurrence with full attention are becoming the dominant paradigm for efficient language models, yet it remains unclear whether adding attention homogenizes model behavior across different recurrence mechanisms. We provide the first quantitative characterization of the \\emph{equalizer effect}: adding attention layers to GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2, and Mamba2-Attn at 1.3B scale reduces cross-architecture performance variance by 79\\% on LAMBADA (std 0.024$\\to$0.005, Spearman $\\rho$=$-$1.0, $p$=0.0083), with convergence strength scaling continuously with task retrieval demand. Per-example analysis reveals an \\emph{equalizer paradox}: despite aggregate convergence, the sample subsets affected by attention are almost entirely non-overlapping across architectures (pairwise Jaccard $<$ 0.10). Mechanistic analysis shows each architecture's attention layers learn distinct patterns---long-range distributed (GDN), last-sentence concentrated (RetNet), hierarchical split (HGRN2)---that compensate for architecture-specific weaknesses. The effect is scale-dependent: monotonically complete at 1.3B but exhibiting a non-monotonic rebound at 340M due to capacity bottleneck. These findings demonstrate that aggregate benchmark convergence can mask fundamentally different per-example behaviors, with implications for architecture selection in deployment.\n29\t\\end{abstract}\n30\t\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\n33\tThe efficient language modeling landscape has shifted toward hybrid architectures that combine linear recurrent layers with full softmax attention \\cite{wang2025mamba,de2024griffin,dao2024transformers}. These hybrids achieve competitive performance with pure Transformers while offering subquadratic inference cost. A natural question arises: as attention layers are added to different recurrence mechanisms, do the resulting models converge to similar behavior?\n34\t\n35\tWang et al.\\ \\cite{wang2025mamba} trained a comprehensive model zoo of 72 hybrid models spanning three recurrence mechanisms (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), multiple scales (340M, 1.3B), and varying attention-to-recurrence ratios (0 to 24 attention layers per recurrence layer). They qualitatively noted that ``the performance gap narrows'' with more attention, but did not provide quantitative analysis of this convergence or investigate its mechanism.\n36\t\n37\tWe conduct the first systematic, quantitative study of this convergence phenomenon and uncover a surprising paradox. Our analysis reveals three key findings:\n38\t\n39\t\\paragraph{Claim 1: Task-Specific Convergence.} The convergence effect is not uniform across tasks but scales continuously with retrieval demand. On LAMBADA, a pure last-word retrieval task, cross-architecture variance drops 79\\% as attention increases ($\\rho$=$-$1.0). On ARC-Easy, which includes substantial factual retrieval, the effect is suggestive ($\\rho$=$-$0.85 after per-example filtering). On reasoning-heavy tasks (HellaSwag, WinoGrande, MMLU), convergence is absent.\n40\t\n41\t\\paragraph{Claim 2: Scale-Dependent Convergence.} At 1.3B parameters, convergence is monotonically complete. At 340M, a non-monotonic rebound occurs at high attention ratios due to a capacity bottleneck: attention parameters consume $\\sim$31\\% of the model budget, compressing the recurrence core's effective capacity.\n42\t\n43\t\\paragraph{Claim 3: The Equalizer Paradox.} Despite aggregate convergence, attention acts on almost entirely different sample subsets per architecture (pairwise Jaccard $<$ 0.10). Each architecture's attention learns distinct compensatory patterns aligned with the recurrence core's specific weaknesses. Convergence emerges from complementary, not redundant, mechanisms.\n44\t\n45\tThese findings have practical implications: even when aggregate benchmarks suggest architectural equivalence, the \\emph{types} of inputs each model handles well remain architecture-specific, making architecture choice consequential for deployment on specific input distributions.\n46\t\n47\t\\section{Background}\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{Linear Recurrent Models.} Recent efficient alternatives to Transformers include models based on linear recurrence: GatedDeltaNet uses a gated delta rule for associative memory \\cite{yang2024gated}, RetNet employs linear attention with exponential decay \\cite{sun2023retentive}, HGRN2 uses hierarchical gated recurrence at multiple timescales \\cite{qin2024hgrn2}, and Mamba2 uses selective state spaces with a data-dependent selection mechanism \\cite{dao2024transformers}.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Hybrid Architectures.} Interleaving these recurrent layers with standard softmax attention layers creates hybrids that combine subquadratic recurrence with the expressivity of full attention. The attention-to-recurrence ratio---the number of attention layers per recurrence layer---is the key design parameter.\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Wang et al.\\ Model Zoo.} Wang et al.\\ \\cite{wang2025mamba} systematically trained 72 models spanning GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, and HGRN2 at 340M and 1.3B parameters with attention ratios $\\in \\{0, 1, 3, 6, 8, 24\\}$. All models were trained on the same data with controlled hyperparameters. We use their publicly available checkpoints as our experimental substrate, supplemented by a community-reproduced Mamba2-Attn hybrid.\n54\t\n55\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Models.} We evaluate all 1.3B models from the Wang et al.\\ zoo (3 architectures $\\times$ 6 attention ratios = 18 models), the full 340M set (10 models), and a Mamba2-Attn 1.3B hybrid from community reproduction. Total: 29 models.\n58\t\n59\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} We evaluate on six benchmarks spanning a range of retrieval demand: LAMBADA (last-word prediction, high retrieval), ARC-Easy and ARC-Challenge (science QA, medium retrieval), HellaSwag (sentence completion, low retrieval), WinoGrande (coreference, low-medium), and MMLU (knowledge, low retrieval). Retrieval demand classifications follow the BIG-bench taxonomy \\cite{srivastava2023beyond}.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{Metrics.} For each benchmark and attention ratio, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation of accuracy across all architectures at 1.3B scale. We measure convergence strength via the Spearman rank correlation ($\\rho$) between attention ratio and cross-architecture std. Per-example analysis uses pairwise Jaccard similarity of helped/hurt sample sets across architectures.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.} All evaluations use \\texttt{lm-evaluation-harness} \\cite{gao2024framework} with \\texttt{--log\\_samples} to obtain per-example predictions. Total compute: $\\sim$26.5 GPU-hours on A100.\n64\t\n65\t\\section{Results}\n66\t\n67\t\\subsection{Task-Specific Convergence Spectrum}\n68\t\\label{sec:task-specificity}\n69\t\n70\tTable~\\ref{tab:convergence} presents the cross-architecture convergence analysis across six benchmarks at 1.3B scale. Rather than applying a binary retrieval/reasoning taxonomy, we examine the relationship between convergence strength and retrieval demand as a continuous spectrum.\n71\t\n72\tLAMBADA shows perfect monotonic convergence ($\\rho$=$-$1.0, permutation $p$=0.0083): cross-architecture std decreases from 0.024 at ratio=0 to 0.005 at ratio=24, a 79\\% reduction. ARC-Easy shows a suggestive relationship ($\\rho$=$-$0.70, $p$=0.117), consistent with its mixture of retrieval-heavy and reasoning-heavy items. The remaining benchmarks show no systematic convergence ($|\\rho| \\leq 0.30$).\n73\t\n74\tThis continuous spectrum---rather than a dichotomy---is consistent with the hypothesis that attention's convergence-inducing effect is mediated by the degree to which a task requires key-value retrieval, the mechanism attention directly implements.\n75\t\n76\t\\begin{table}[t]\n77\t\\centering\n78\t\\small\n79\t\\begin{tabular}{lcrcc}\n80\t\\toprule\n81\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Ret.\\ Dem.} & \\textbf{$\\rho$} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Std$_0$$\\to$Std$_{24}$} \\\\\n82\t\\midrule\n83\tLAMBADA & High & $-$1.00 & 0.008 & .024$\\to$.005 \\\\\n84\tARC-Easy & Med-High & $-$0.70 & 0.117 & .031$\\to$.018 \\\\\n85\tARC-Chall. & Medium & $-$0.30 & 0.347 & .022$\\to$.019 \\\\\n86\tHellaSwag & Low & $-$0.10 & 0.782 & .018$\\to$.017 \\\\\n87\tWinoGrande & Low-Med & $+$0.05 & 0.912 & .015$\\to$.016 \\\\\n88\tMMLU & Low & $+$0.10 & 0.782 & .028$\\to$.027 \\\\\n89\t\\bottomrule\n90\t\\end{tabular}\n91\t\\caption{Cross-architecture convergence by benchmark at 1.3B scale. $\\rho$: Spearman correlation between attention ratio and cross-architecture std. Retrieval demand follows BIG-bench taxonomy. Std$_0$ and Std$_{24}$: cross-architecture std at attention ratio 0 and 24 respectively.}\n92\t\\label{tab:convergence}\n93\t\\end{table}\n94\t\n95\t\\subsection{Per-Example Analysis Confirms and Refines the Spectrum}\n96\t\\label{sec:per-example}\n97\t\n98\tAggregate convergence metrics could reflect uniform effects or heterogeneous per-example dynamics. We conduct per-example analysis on four benchmarks using 23 models (1.3B and 340M) with per-sample prediction logs.\n99\t\n100\tTable~\\ref{tab:per-example} reveals that per-example analysis both confirms non-convergence on reasoning tasks and strengthens convergence evidence on retrieval tasks. HellaSwag shows systematic but architecture-specific patterns at the per-example level---attention changes \\emph{which} samples each architecture gets correct, but in non-converging ways. ARC-Easy convergence strengthens from $\\rho$=$-$0.70 to $\\rho$=$-$0.85 after filtering to retrieval-heavy items, establishing it as a second anchor point. ARC-Challenge shows no systematic per-example pattern.\n101\t\n102\tThe low pairwise Jaccard similarities (0.06--0.31) across benchmarks indicate that even where convergence occurs, the specific samples affected differ substantially across architectures.\n103\t\n104\t\\begin{table}[t]\n105\t\\centering\n106\t\\small\n107\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccl}\n108\t\\toprule\n109\t\\textbf{Bench.} & \\textbf{$\\rho_{\\text{agg}}$} & \\textbf{$\\rho_{\\text{per-ex}}$} & \\textbf{Jacc.} & \\textbf{Pattern} \\\\\n110\t\\midrule\n111\tLAMBADA & $-$1.00 & $-$1.00 & 0.06 & Arch-specific \\\\\n112\tARC-E & $-$0.70 & $-$0.85 & 0.12 & Retrieval subset \\\\\n113\tARC-C & $-$0.30 & $-$0.25 & 0.31 & No pattern \\\\\n114\tHellaSwag & $-$0.10 & $-$0.08 & 0.22 & Systematic, non-conv. \\\\\n115\t\\bottomrule\n116\t\\end{tabular}\n117\t\\caption{Per-example convergence metrics. $\\rho_{\\text{agg}}$: aggregate Spearman correlation. $\\rho_{\\text{per-ex}}$: per-example filtered correlation. Jacc.: mean pairwise Jaccard of helped/hurt sets. 23 models evaluated across 18,743 total samples.}\n118\t\\label{tab:per-example}\n119\t\\end{table}\n120\t\n121\t\\subsection{Mechanistic Analysis: Sample Features and Attention Patterns}\n122\t\\label{sec:mechanism}\n123\t\n124\tTo understand \\emph{why} architectures are helped on different samples, we characterize the LAMBADA helped/hurt sets using four linguistic features and analyze the learned attention patterns.\n125\t\n126\t\\paragraph{Sample Feature Characterization.} Table~\\ref{tab:features} shows that three features significantly distinguish helped from hurt samples ($p < 0.001$): target token frequency, context length, and last-sentence perplexity. Critically, the \\emph{feature profiles differ across architectures} ($p$=0.0003): GDN's helped samples concentrate on rare tokens in long contexts (AUC=0.71 for token frequency), RetNet's on high-perplexity last sentences (AUC=0.69), and HGRN2's on medium-frequency tokens (AUC=0.64). These profiles align with known recurrence mechanisms: GDN's delta rule for associative recall benefits from attention's precise rare-token retrieval; RetNet's exponential decay benefits from attention's disambiguation in complex local contexts; HGRN2's hierarchical gating benefits from attention's frequency-band expansion.\n127\t\n128\t\\begin{table}[t]\n129\t\\centering\n130\t\\small\n131\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n132\t\\toprule\n133\t\\textbf{Feature} & \\textbf{GDN} & \\textbf{RetNet} & \\textbf{HGRN2} & \\textbf{$p_{\\text{cross}}$} \\\\\n134\t\\midrule\n135\tToken freq & 0.71 & 0.58 & 0.64 & 0.0012 \\\\\n136\tContext len & 0.67 & 0.55 & 0.57 & 0.0089 \\\\\n137\tLast-sent PPL & 0.56 & 0.69 & 0.61 & 0.0003 \\\\\n138\tDomain entropy & 0.54 & 0.53 & 0.55 & 0.31 \\\\\n139\t\\bottomrule\n140\t\\end{tabular}\n141\t\\caption{AUC for helped/hurt classification by feature and architecture on LAMBADA. $p_{\\text{cross}}$: significance of cross-architecture profile difference. Three features achieve $p < 0.001$ overall; Cohen's $d$=0.72 for the best feature.}\n142\t\\label{tab:features}\n143\t\\end{table}\n144\t\n145\t\\paragraph{Attention Pattern Analysis.}\n146\tTable~\\ref{tab:attention} presents the learned attention patterns from the h3-1 configuration (3 recurrence: 1 attention). Each architecture's attention layers develop distinct focus patterns: GDN attention distributes over long-range context tokens (entropy=3.41), RetNet attention concentrates on last-sentence tokens (entropy=2.18), and HGRN2 attention splits hierarchically between local and mid-range positions (entropy=2.89). These patterns correlate with the helped/hurt sample identity (mean $r$=0.54, range 0.48--0.61, all $p < 0.001$), establishing a directed mechanistic link from recurrence weakness $\\to$ compensatory attention pattern $\\to$ architecture-specific sample effects $\\to$ aggregate convergence.\n147\t\n148\tCross-architecture attention similarity is notably low (mean cosine=0.23, significantly below random baseline $p < 0.003$; Table~\\ref{tab:attn-sim}), confirming that the same attention architecture learns fundamentally different behaviors depending on its recurrence backbone.\n149\t\n150\t\\begin{table}[t]\n151\t\\centering\n152\t\\small\n153\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n154\t\\toprule\n155\t\\textbf{Arch.} & \\textbf{Focus} & \\textbf{$r$ (h/h)} & \\textbf{Entropy} \\\\\n156\t\\midrule\n157\tGDN & Long-range dist. & 0.52 & 3.41 \\\\\n158\tRetNet & Last-sent conc. & 0.61 & 2.18 \\\\\n159\tHGRN2 & Hierarchical & 0.48 & 2.89 \\\\\n160\t\\bottomrule\n161\t\\end{tabular}\n162\t\\caption{Attention pattern analysis on LAMBADA. $r$ (h/h): correlation between attention weights and helped/hurt identity. All $p < 5 \\times 10^{-4}$.}\n163\t\\label{tab:attention}\n164\t\\end{table}\n165\t\n166\t\\begin{table}[t]\n167\t\\centering\n168\t\\small\n169\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n170\t\\toprule\n171\t\\textbf{Pair} & \\textbf{Cosine sim} & \\textbf{$p$ vs.\\ random} \\\\\n172\t\\midrule\n173\tGDN--RetNet & 0.19 & $<$0.001 \\\\\n174\tGDN--HGRN2 & 0.27 & 0.003 \\\\\n175\tRetNet--HGRN2 & 0.24 & 0.001 \\\\\n176\t\\bottomrule\n177\t\\end{tabular}\n178\t\\caption{Cross-architecture attention similarity (cosine). All pairs significantly \\emph{below} random baseline, indicating architecture-specific attention behavior.}\n179\t\\label{tab:attn-sim}\n180\t\\end{table}\n181\t\n182\t\\subsection{Architecture Generalization}\n183\t\\label{sec:expansion}\n184\t\n185\tTo test whether the equalizer effect generalizes beyond the three architectures in the Wang et al.\\ zoo, we evaluate a Mamba2-Attn hybrid (selective state space + attention) from community reproduction.\n186\t\n187\tTable~\\ref{tab:expansion} shows that Mamba2-Attn replicates the convergence pattern on LAMBADA ($\\rho$=$-$0.90, std reduction 71\\%), with Jaccard overlap $\\leq$ 0.07 against all other architectures. The slightly weaker convergence ($\\rho$=$-$0.90 vs.\\ $-$1.0) may reflect differences in attention integration (interleaved vs.\\ stacked). With four architectures spanning gated delta rules, exponential decay, hierarchical gating, and selective state spaces, the equalizer effect appears to be a general property of attention + recurrence hybrids rather than an artifact of three specific architectures.\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[t]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\small\n192\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n193\t\\toprule\n194\t\\textbf{Arch.} & \\textbf{Recurrence} & \\textbf{$\\rho$} & \\textbf{Std$\\downarrow$} & \\textbf{Jacc.} \\\\\n195\t\\midrule\n196\tGDN & Gated delta & $-$1.00 & 79\\% & --- \\\\\n197\tRetNet & Lin.\\ attn + decay & $-$1.00 & 79\\% & 0.06 \\\\\n198\tHGRN2 & Hier.\\ gating & $-$1.00 & 79\\% & 0.04--0.08 \\\\\n199\tMamba2 & Sel.\\ state space & $-$0.90 & 71\\% & 0.04--0.07 \\\\\n200\t\\bottomrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"8e941d9f-a15f-4753-80ff-84f3006eb543","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:18:21.007Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NYEw4p99kgdQhuZQRZyw5R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HDVHre4u8HsGwFzqe3qAvP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T135121/best/paper.tex","offset":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9852,"cache_read_input_tokens":19496,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9852,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"16910ede-9737-4ba9-877b-a15cf76b24c4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HDVHre4u8HsGwFzqe3qAvP","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Point Est.} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & \\textbf{N} \\\\\n201\t\\midrule\n202\tStd reduction (3 arch)            & 79\\%   & [0.65, 0.88] & 10{,}000 \\\\\n203\tStd reduction (GDN-excl., 2 arch) & 62\\%   & [0.42, 0.78] & 10{,}000 \\\\\n204\tStd reduction (GDN-excl., 3 arch) & 68\\%   & [0.55, 0.82] & 10{,}000 \\\\\n205\tSpearman $\\rho$ (LAMBADA)         & $-$1.0 & [$-$1.0, $-$0.90] & 10{,}000 \\\\\n206\tPer-example Jaccard (mean pw.)    & 0.07   & [0.04, 0.11] & 10{,}000 \\\\\n207\t\\bottomrule\n208\t\\end{tabular}\n209\t\\end{table}\n210\t\n211\t\\paragraph{Multiple-comparison correction.}\n212\tTable~\\ref{tab:multicomp} reports raw, Bonferroni-corrected, and FDR-corrected $p$-values across all six benchmarks.\n213\tLAMBADA survives Bonferroni correction at $\\alpha{=}0.05$ (corrected $p{=}0.048$).\n214\tARC-Easy and MMLU do not survive correction, consistent with their classification as suggestive rather than significant.\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{table}[t]\n217\t\\centering\n218\t\\small\n219\t\\caption{Multiple-comparison corrected $p$-values across benchmarks.}\n220\t\\label{tab:multicomp}\n221\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n222\t\\toprule\n223\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Raw $p$} & \\textbf{Bonferroni} & \\textbf{FDR} & \\textbf{Sig.\\ ($\\alpha{=}0.05$)} \\\\\n224\t\\midrule\n225\tLAMBADA       & 0.008 & 0.048 & 0.024 & Yes \\\\\n226\tARC-Easy      & 0.117 & 0.702 & 0.234 & No \\\\\n227\tMMLU          & 0.117 & 0.702 & 0.234 & No \\\\\n228\tARC-Chall.    & 0.347 & 1.000 & 0.416 & No \\\\\n229\tWinoGrande    & 0.608 & 1.000 & 0.608 & No \\\\\n230\tHellaSwag     & 0.742 & 1.000 & 0.742 & No \\\\\n231\t\\bottomrule\n232\t\\end{tabular}\n233\t\\end{table}\n234\t\n235\t\\paragraph{Effect sizes.}\n236\tThe convergence effect is large by conventional standards (Table~\\ref{tab:effect-sizes}): Cohen's $d{=}1.82$ for the h0-vs-h24 cross-architecture std comparison and $\\eta^2{=}0.74$ for attention ratio as a predictor of cross-architecture variance (ANOVA).\n237\tThese values place the equalizer effect well above the threshold of ``statistically significant but trivially small.''\n238\t\n239\t\\begin{table}[t]\n240\t\\centering\n241\t\\small\n242\t\\caption{Effect sizes for primary comparisons.}\n243\t\\label{tab:effect-sizes}\n244\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n245\t\\toprule\n246\t\\textbf{Comparison} & \\textbf{Cohen's $d$} & \\textbf{$\\eta^2$} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n247\t\\midrule\n248\th0 vs h24 cross-arch std           & 1.82 & --- & Very large \\\\\n249\tAttention ratio $\\to$ std (ANOVA)  & ---  & 0.74 & Very large \\\\\n250\tHelped vs hurt PPL difference      & 0.55 & --- & Medium-large \\\\\n251\t\\bottomrule\n252\t\\end{tabular}\n253\t\\end{table}\n254\t\n255\t% -------------------------------------------------------\n256\t\\subsection{Architecture Expansion: Four Recurrence Mechanisms}\n257\t\\label{sec:arch-expansion}\n258\t\n259\tA potential criticism of the equalizer finding is that $n{=}3$ architectures may be insufficient.\n260\tWe address this by incorporating Mamba2-hybrid models from the Wang et al.\\ model zoo, expanding coverage to four fundamentally distinct recurrence mechanisms: gated delta rule (GDN), exponential decay (RetNet), hierarchical gating (HGRN2), and selective state spaces (Mamba2).\n261\t\n262\tTable~\\ref{tab:four-arch-std} shows that the convergence pattern strengthens with 4 architectures.\n263\tCross-architecture std at h0 increases from 0.024 (3 arch) to 0.031 (4 arch), reflecting Mamba2's distinct baseline, while std at h24-1 remains at 0.005.\n264\tThe Spearman $\\rho$ remains $-$1.0 with an improved exact permutation $p{=}0.003$ (vs.\\ 0.017 with $n{=}3$).\n265\t\n266\t\\begin{table}[t]\n267\t\\centering\n268\t\\small\n269\t\\caption{Cross-architecture std with 4 architectures (1.3B, LAMBADA).}\n270\t\\label{tab:four-arch-std}\n271\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n272\t\\toprule\n273\t\\textbf{Attn.\\ Ratio} & \\textbf{N Archs} & \\textbf{Std (4 arch)} & \\textbf{Std (3 arch)} \\\\\n274\t\\midrule\n275\th0 (pure)  & 4 & 0.031 & 0.024 \\\\\n276\th3-1       & 4 & 0.012 & 0.010 \\\\\n277\th6-1       & 4 & 0.008 & 0.007 \\\\\n278\th12-1      & 4 & 0.006 & 0.005 \\\\\n279\th24-1      & 4 & 0.005 & 0.005 \\\\\n280\t\\bottomrule\n281\t\\end{tabular}\n282\t\\end{table}\n283\t\n284\tTable~\\ref{tab:jaccard-matrix} presents the full pairwise Jaccard matrix for helped/hurt sets across all four architectures.\n285\tAll values are below 0.10, confirming that Mamba2's selective state-space mechanism produces yet another distinct pattern of architecture-specific effects, consistent with the equalizer paradox.\n286\t\n287\t\\begin{table}[t]\n288\t\\centering\n289\t\\small\n290\t\\caption{Pairwise Jaccard similarity of helped/hurt sets (4 architectures, 1.3B LAMBADA).}\n291\t\\label{tab:jaccard-matrix}\n292\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n293\t\\toprule\n294\t         & \\textbf{GDN} & \\textbf{RetNet} & \\textbf{HGRN2} & \\textbf{Mamba2} \\\\\n295\t\\midrule\n296\tGDN    & --- & 0.05 & 0.07 & 0.06 \\\\\n297\tRetNet & 0.05 & --- & 0.08 & 0.07 \\\\\n298\tHGRN2  & 0.07 & 0.08 & --- & 0.09 \\\\\n299\tMamba2 & 0.06 & 0.07 & 0.09 & --- \\\\\n300\t\\bottomrule\n301\t\\end{tabular}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\t\n305\t% -------------------------------------------------------\n306\t\\subsection{The Equalizer Paradox: Aggregate Convergence Through Per-Example Divergence}\n307\t\\label{sec:paradox}\n308\t\n309\tThe convergence--divergence bridge analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:bridge}) jointly tracks aggregate and per-example metrics as a function of attention ratio, directly quantifying the equalizer paradox.\n310\t\n311\tAs attention increases from h0 to h24-1: (1) cross-architecture std monotonically decreases from 0.024 to 0.005 (aggregate convergence); (2) mean per-example effect magnitude increases from 0 to 0.055 (each architecture's helped/hurt effects grow stronger); and (3) pairwise Jaccard decreases from baseline to 0.05 (helped/hurt sets become \\emph{more} architecture-specific, not less).\n312\t\n313\tThe correlation between aggregate convergence and per-example divergence is $\\rho{=}{-}0.94$ ($p{=}0.005$).\n314\tThis strong negative correlation quantifies the paradox: more attention produces both more aggregate convergence and more per-example divergence, because the same mechanism---compensation for architecture-specific blind spots---drives both simultaneously.\n315\t\n316\t\\begin{table}[t]\n317\t\\centering\n318\t\\small\n319\t\\caption{Convergence--divergence co-evolution across attention ratios (1.3B, LAMBADA). Cross-Arch Std measures aggregate convergence; Mean $|\\text{Effect}|$ and Jaccard measure per-example divergence.}\n320\t\\label{tab:bridge}\n321\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n322\t\\toprule\n323\t\\textbf{Attn.\\ Ratio} & \\textbf{Std} & \\textbf{Mean $|\\text{Eff}|$} & \\textbf{Jaccard} & \\textbf{GDN Net} & \\textbf{HGRN2 Net} \\\\\n324\t\\midrule\n325\th0    & 0.024 & 0.000 & ---  & 0.00  & 0.00 \\\\\n326\th3-1  & 0.018 & 0.032 & 0.09 & $-$0.15 & $+$0.08 \\\\\n327\th6-1  & 0.012 & 0.041 & 0.07 & $-$0.12 & $+$0.11 \\\\\n328\th12-1 & 0.007 & 0.048 & 0.06 & $-$0.09 & $+$0.14 \\\\\n329\th24-1 & 0.005 & 0.055 & 0.05 & $-$0.14 & $+$0.15 \\\\\n330\t\\bottomrule\n331\t\\end{tabular}\n332\t\\end{table}\n333\t\n334\tThe net effect asymmetry reveals another dimension of the paradox: GDN and RetNet show net negative effects on LAMBADA (attention hurts more samples than it helps), while HGRN2 shows net positive effects, yet all three converge to similar aggregate accuracy.\n335\tThis asymmetry is stable across attention ratios, reflecting differences in how well each architecture's recurrence mechanism is suited to LAMBADA's retrieval structure.\n336\t\n337\t\n338\t% -------------------------------------------------------\n339\t\\subsection{Mechanistic Analysis: Perplexity-Based Compensation}\n340\t\\label{sec:mechanism}\n341\t\n342\tHaving established the equalizer paradox, we investigate its mechanism.\n343\tWe compute baseline perplexity (pure recurrent model, no attention) for each LAMBADA sample per architecture, then test whether helped and hurt samples differ in baseline perplexity.\n344\t\n345\t\\paragraph{Perplexity differentiates helped vs.\\ hurt.}\n346\tTable~\\ref{tab:ppl-mechanism} shows that across all three architectures, samples helped by attention have significantly higher baseline perplexity than hurt samples (all $p < 0.001$, Cohen's $d \\in [0.51, 0.62]$).\n347\tThe direction is universal: attention rescues high-uncertainty predictions where the recurrent state has lost or blurred the relevant information.\n348\t\n349\t\\begin{table}[t]\n350\t\\centering\n351\t\\small\n352\t\\caption{Baseline perplexity differentiates helped vs.\\ hurt samples (1.3B, LAMBADA).}\n353\t\\label{tab:ppl-mechanism}\n354\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n355\t\\toprule\n356\t\\textbf{Arch.} & \\textbf{Helped PPL} & \\textbf{Hurt PPL} & \\textbf{$p$ (MW)} & \\textbf{Cohen's $d$} & \\textbf{Direction} \\\\\n357\t\\midrule\n358\tGDN    & 245.3 & 89.7  & 0.0003 & 0.58 & Helped = high unc. \\\\\n359\tHGRN2  & 198.6 & 72.4  & 0.0008 & 0.51 & Helped = high unc. \\\\\n360\tRetNet & 267.1 & 95.3  & 0.0002 & 0.62 & Helped = high unc. \\\\\n361\t\\bottomrule\n362\t\\end{tabular}\n363\t\\end{table}\n364\t\n365\t\\paragraph{Architecture-specific uncertainty profiles.}\n366\tCrucially, which samples are high-uncertainty is architecture-specific (Table~\\ref{tab:ppl-cross}).\n367\tCross-architecture perplexity ranking correlations are moderate ($\\rho \\approx 0.35$), and helped-set overlap among the top-20\\% perplexity samples is low (16--20\\%).\n368\tEach recurrence mechanism---gated delta rule, exponential decay, hierarchical gating---has different information retention blind spots, producing different perplexity profiles.\n369\t\n370\tThis provides the complete mechanistic explanation for the equalizer paradox: (a) attention compensates for high-uncertainty predictions (universal direction), but (b) which predictions are high-uncertainty is architecture-specific (low overlap), yielding (c) aggregate convergence through non-overlapping per-example effects.\n371\t\n372\t\\begin{table}[t]\n373\t\\centering\n374\t\\small\n375\t\\caption{Cross-architecture perplexity profile comparison (1.3B, LAMBADA).}\n376\t\\label{tab:ppl-cross}\n377\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n378\t\\toprule\n379\t\\textbf{Arch Pair} & \\textbf{PPL Rank $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Helped Overlap} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n380\t\\midrule\n381\tGDN--HGRN2  & 0.38 & 0.20 & Moderate PPL corr., low overlap \\\\\n382\tGDN--RetNet & 0.32 & 0.16 & Low PPL corr., very low overlap \\\\\n383\tHGRN2--RetNet & 0.34 & 0.19 & Moderate PPL corr., low overlap \\\\\n384\t\\bottomrule\n385\t\\end{tabular}\n386\t\\end{table}\n387\t\n388\t\n389\t% -------------------------------------------------------\n390\t\\subsection{Attention Weight Analysis}\n391\t\\label{sec:attention-weights}\n392\t\n393\tWe extract attention weight patterns from h3-1 hybrid models (3 attention layers out of 24) for GDN, RetNet, and HGRN2 at 1.3B scale to directly observe how attention compensates for recurrent blind spots.\n394\t\n395\t\\paragraph{Attention entropy differentiates helped vs.\\ hurt.}\n396\tTable~\\ref{tab:attn-entropy} shows that attention patterns are significantly more concentrated (lower entropy) on helped samples than on hurt samples across all architectures (all $p \\leq 0.003$).\n397\tThe correlation between attention concentration and helped/hurt identity is $r \\approx 0.38$ (mean across architectures).\n398\tOn helped samples, attention focuses on specific context positions containing the information lost by the recurrent state; on hurt samples, attention is diffuse because the recurrent state already encodes the necessary information.\n399\t\n400\t\\begin{table}[t]\n401\t\\centering\n402\t\\small\n403\t\\caption{Attention pattern metrics for helped vs.\\ hurt samples (1.3B, h3-1, LAMBADA).}\n404\t\\label{tab:attn-entropy}\n405\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n406\t\\toprule\n407\t\\textbf{Arch.} & \\textbf{Helped Entropy} & \\textbf{Hurt Entropy} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Conc.--Effect $r$} \\\\\n408\t\\midrule\n409\tGDN    & 2.31 & 3.14 & 0.001 & 0.38 \\\\\n410\tHGRN2  & 2.48 & 3.22 & 0.003 & 0.34 \\\\\n411\tRetNet & 2.19 & 3.08 & 0.002 & 0.41 \\\\\n412\t\\bottomrule\n413\t\\end{tabular}\n414\t\\end{table}\n415\t\n416\t\\paragraph{Architecture-specific compensation targets.}\n417\tTable~\\ref{tab:attn-positions} reveals that different architectures' attention layers focus on different context distances, consistent with the perplexity-based mechanism.\n418\tGDN's attention concentrates on nearby positions (3--7 tokens), compensating for its delta-rule's short-term decay.\n419\tRetNet's attention reaches further back (8--18 tokens), compensating for exponential decay's longer-range loss.\n420\tHGRN2 shows variable distances (4--12 tokens), reflecting its hierarchical gate structure.\n421\tCross-architecture attention pattern similarity is low (0.22), directly visualizing the architecture-specific compensation identified in the perplexity analysis.\n422\t\n423\t\\begin{table}[t]\n424\t\\centering\n425\t\\small\n426\t\\caption{Attention focus position analysis across architectures (1.3B, h3-1, helped samples).}\n427\t\\label{tab:attn-positions}\n428\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n429\t\\toprule\n430\t\\textbf{Arch.} & \\textbf{Modal Dist.\\ (helped)} & \\textbf{Modal Dist.\\ (hurt)} & \\textbf{Compensation Target} \\\\\n431\t\\midrule\n432\tGDN    & 3--7 tokens  & diffuse & Recent context (delta-rule decay) \\\\\n433\tRetNet & 8--18 tokens & diffuse & Mid-range (exp.\\ decay) \\\\\n434\tHGRN2  & 4--12 tokens & diffuse & Variable (hier.\\ gate-dep.) \\\\\n435\t\\bottomrule\n436\t\\end{tabular}\n437\t\\end{table}\n438\t\n439\t\n440\t% -------------------------------------------------------\n441\t\\subsection{Scale-Dependent Convergence and the 340M Rebound}\n442\t\\label{sec:scale}\n443\t\n444\tWe analyze the non-monotonic rebound at 340M to establish Claim~2 and provide a mechanistic explanation for scale dependency.\n445\t\n446\tAt 1.3B, the equalizer effect is monotonically complete: cross-architecture std decreases at every attention ratio increment (Table~\\ref{tab:scale-comparison}).\n447\tAt 340M, convergence is partial (51\\% std reduction, peaking at h6-1) with a rebound at h12-1 and above.\n448\t\n449\t\\begin{table}[t]\n450\t\\centering\n451\t\\small\n452\t\\caption{Scale comparison of convergence completeness (LAMBADA).}\n453\t\\label{tab:scale-comparison}\n454\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n455\t\\toprule\n456\t\\textbf{Scale} & \\textbf{Monotonic?} & \\textbf{Std Red.\\ \\%} & \\textbf{Rebound} & \\textbf{Mechanism} \\\\\n457\t\\midrule\n458\t1.3B & Yes              & 79\\% & None   & Sufficient capacity \\\\\n459\t340M & No (h12+ reb.)   & 51\\% & h12-1  & Capacity bottleneck \\\\\n460\t\\bottomrule\n461\t\\end{tabular}\n462\t\\end{table}\n463\t\n464\t\\paragraph{Rebound sample characterization.}\n465\tTable~\\ref{tab:rebound-samples} characterizes the 127 samples that exhibit rebound behavior (correct at intermediate attention but incorrect at high attention at 340M).\n466\tThese samples are overwhelmingly difficult: 78\\% fall in the top quartile of baseline perplexity (mean PPL = 412.5).\n467\tAt 1.3B, the same samples achieve 71\\% accuracy at h24-1, confirming that sufficient capacity resolves the bottleneck.\n468\t\n469\tThe mechanism is parameter reallocation: at 340M, shifting parameters from recurrent to attention layers beyond the h12-1 threshold degrades recurrent state quality on the hardest samples---precisely those that depend most on good recurrent representations.\n470\tAt 1.3B, total capacity is sufficient for both pathways to operate effectively.\n471\t\n472\t\\begin{table}[t]\n473\t\\centering\n474\t\\small\n475\t\\caption{340M rebound sample characterization (LAMBADA).}\n476\t\\label{tab:rebound-samples}\n477\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n478\t\\toprule\n479\t\\textbf{Subset} & \\textbf{N} & \\textbf{Mean PPL} & \\textbf{340M h6} & \\textbf{340M h24} & \\textbf{1.3B h24} \\\\\n480\t\\midrule\n481\tRebound         & 127  & 412.5 & 0.52 & 0.34 & 0.71 \\\\\n482\tMono.-improving & 389  & 156.3 & 0.41 & 0.58 & 0.62 \\\\\n483\tStable-correct  & 1847 & 45.2  & 0.92 & 0.91 & 0.95 \\\\\n484\tStable-wrong    & 698  & 523.7 & 0.08 & 0.06 & 0.31 \\\\\n485\t\\bottomrule\n486\t\\end{tabular}\n487\t\\end{table}\n488\t\n489\tThis analysis transforms the scale dependency from a limitation (``only two scale points'') into positive evidence: the 340M rebound on precisely the hardest samples is exactly what the perplexity-based mechanism predicts when capacity is insufficient.\n490\t\n491\t\n492\t% ============================================================\n493\t% 5  DISCUSSION\n494\t% ============================================================\n495\t\\section{Discussion}\n496\t\n497\t\\paragraph{Implications for architecture selection.}\n498\tThe equalizer paradox implies that aggregate benchmark comparisons between hybrid architectures can be misleading.\n499\tTwo architectures achieving identical LAMBADA accuracy may be succeeding on largely disjoint subsets of examples.\n500\tFor applications where reliability on specific input types matters (e.g., particular domains or linguistic constructions), per-example analysis may be more informative than aggregate metrics.\n501\t\n502\t\\paragraph{Implications for ensembling.}\n503\tThe low pairwise Jaccard overlap ($<$\\,0.10) suggests that ensembles of different hybrid architectures could yield complementary error profiles.\n504\tThe perplexity-based mechanism further suggests that architecture-specific blind spots are predictable from the recurrence mechanism, enabling principled ensemble construction.\n505\t\n506\t\\paragraph{The role of retrieval demand.}\n507\tThe dose-response relationship between retrieval demand and convergence strength suggests that the equalizer effect is fundamentally about information retrieval from context.\n508\tAttention provides a uniform mechanism for direct context access; when the task requires this access (high retrieval demand), attention equalizes across architectures because it bypasses recurrence-specific information loss.\n509\tWhen the task requires compositional reasoning rather than retrieval, attention provides no uniform advantage, and architecture-specific differences persist.\n510\t\n511\t\\paragraph{Capacity as a necessary condition.}\n512\tThe 340M rebound establishes that the equalizer mechanism has a capacity prerequisite.\n513\tThis has practical implications: practitioners using smaller hybrid models should expect greater sensitivity to architecture choice, even with attention layers.\n514\t\n515\t\n516\t% ============================================================\n517\t% 6  LIMITATIONS\n518\t% ============================================================\n519\t\\section{Limitations}\n520\t\n521\t\\textbf{Scale coverage.}\n522\tWe observe only two scales (340M, 1.3B).\n523\tWhile the mechanistic analysis of the 340M rebound provides an explanatory framework, we cannot empirically verify whether convergence strengthens further at 7B+ parameters.\n524\t\n525\t\\textbf{Architecture sample.}\n526\tFour architectures, while spanning the major families of linear recurrence, do not exhaust the space.\n527\tOur findings may not generalize to architectures with substantially different state management (e.g., RWKV's channel-mixing).\n528\t\n529\t\\textbf{Benchmark coverage.}\n530\tOnly LAMBADA shows statistically significant convergence after multiple-comparison correction.\n531\tThe dose-response framing relies on benchmarks that do not individually reach significance.\n532\tWhile the continuous gradient ($\\rho{=}{-}0.89$) is suggestive, stronger evidence would require retrieval-heavy benchmarks that independently replicate the LAMBADA finding.\n533\t\n534\t\\textbf{Retrieval demand quantification.}\n535\tThe retrieval demand index is derived from existing task taxonomies rather than computed from first principles.\n536\tA more rigorous operationalization of ``retrieval demand'' would strengthen the dose-response claim.\n537\t\n538\t\\textbf{Causal mechanism.}\n539\tOur perplexity-based explanation is correlational.\n540\tWhile it is consistent across architectures and provides strong explanatory coherence, we do not establish causality (e.g., via targeted interventions on attention patterns).\n541\t\n542\t\n543\t% ============================================================\n544\t% 7  CONCLUSION\n545\t% ============================================================\n546\t\\section{Conclusion}\n547\t\n548\tWe have presented the equalizer paradox: a systematic empirical phenomenon in which adding attention layers to hybrid linear recurrent architectures produces aggregate performance convergence through per-example divergence.\n549\tAcross four architectures, five attention ratios, and six benchmarks, we show that convergence strength scales continuously with task retrieval demand ($\\rho{=}{-}0.89$), is scale-dependent (complete at 1.3B, partial with capacity-bottleneck rebound at 340M), and is mechanistically driven by attention compensating for architecture-specific high-uncertainty tokens.\n550\tOn LAMBADA, cross-architecture variance drops 79\\% (Bonferroni-corrected $p{=}0.048$, Cohen's $d{=}1.82$), yet per-example effects are nearly non-overlapping (Jaccard $<$\\,0.10), with convergence and divergence co-evolving at $\\rho{=}{-}0.94$.\n551\tThese findings reveal that benchmark-level parity between hybrid architectures masks fundamentally different processing strategies, with implications for architecture selection, ensembling, and the interpretation of model comparisons.\n552\t\n553\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n554\t\\bibliography{references}\n555\t\n556\t\\end{document}\n557\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"f41a2ea2-d6f1-447f-9c33-6de6f646d2fb","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:18:26.526Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NYEw4p99kgdQhuZQRZyw5R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HJNB9tQV7eR1x7Wu1whiv3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T131014/best/paper.tex","offset":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9852,"cache_read_input_tokens":19496,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9852,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"b64c9546-1d54-4ec3-a303-1d1dd032d3de"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HJNB9tQV7eR1x7Wu1whiv3","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\\bottomrule\n201\t\\end{tabular}\n202\t\\caption{Architecture expansion on LAMBADA. Std$\\downarrow$: std reduction from ratio=0 to ratio=24. Jacc.: range of pairwise Jaccard with other architectures. Four distinct recurrence mechanisms all exhibit the convergence pattern.}\n203\t\\label{tab:expansion}\n204\t\\end{table}\n205\t\n206\t\\subsection{Scale-Dependent Convergence and Capacity Bottleneck}\n207\t\\label{sec:scale}\n208\t\n209\tThe equalizer effect is scale-dependent. At 1.3B, convergence is monotonically complete. At 340M, convergence proceeds up to attention ratio=6 but exhibits a non-monotonic rebound at ratio=8 (cross-arch std rises from 0.009 to 0.014).\n210\t\n211\tTable~\\ref{tab:rebound} characterizes this rebound. At ratio=8, attention parameters constitute $\\sim$31\\% of the 340M model budget (vs.\\ $\\sim$12\\% at 1.3B), creating a capacity bottleneck: the recurrence core loses effective capacity. The 412 samples that become incorrect at 340M ratio=8 are predominantly long-context, high-frequency-token samples that require strong recurrence---73\\% of these samples remain correct at 1.3B ratio=8. This pattern matches capacity bottleneck predictions and rules out training token count as a confound.\n212\t\n213\tThe scale contrast establishes that sufficient model capacity is a prerequisite for attention to \\emph{complement} rather than \\emph{compete with} recurrence.\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{table}[t]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\small\n218\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n219\t\\toprule\n220\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{340M/6} & \\textbf{340M/8} & \\textbf{1.3B/8} \\\\\n221\t\\midrule\n222\tCross-arch std & 0.009 & 0.014 & 0.006 \\\\\n223\tAttn param ratio & 0.23 & 0.31 & 0.12 \\\\\n224\tRebound samples & --- & 412 & 0 \\\\\n225\tRebound features & --- & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Long ctx + high-freq tokens} \\\\\n226\t\\bottomrule\n227\t\\end{tabular}\n228\t\\caption{340M rebound characterization. At ratio=8, attention parameters consume 31\\% of the 340M budget, causing capacity bottleneck. Rebound samples concentrate on recurrence-demanding features.}\n229\t\\label{tab:rebound}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\t\\subsection{The Convergence-Divergence Bridge}\n233\t\\label{sec:bridge}\n234\t\n235\tThe equalizer paradox is best visualized through the co-evolution of aggregate convergence and per-example divergence as attention ratio increases. Table~\\ref{tab:bridge} presents this analysis on LAMBADA.\n236\t\n237\tAs attention ratio increases from 0 to 24, cross-architecture std monotonically decreases (0.024$\\to$0.005) while per-example Jaccard also decreases (0.08$\\to$0.04), indicating that helped/hurt subsets become \\emph{less} overlapping. These trends are strongly anti-correlated ($r$=$-$0.94, $p$=0.005). The net effect asymmetry---GDN and RetNet show net negative effects, HGRN2 shows net positive, with approximately symmetric magnitudes---reveals that convergence emerges not from architectures being ``pulled up'' uniformly but from all being ``pushed toward the middle'' through complementary adjustments on different samples.\n238\t\n239\t\\begin{table}[t]\n240\t\\centering\n241\t\\small\n242\t\\begin{tabular}{rccccc}\n243\t\\toprule\n244\t\\textbf{Ratio} & \\textbf{Std} & \\textbf{Jacc.} & \\textbf{GDN} & \\textbf{Ret} & \\textbf{HGRN2} \\\\\n245\t\\midrule\n246\t0 & .024 & --- & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n247\t1 & .018 & .08 & $-$.03 & $-$.02 & +.03 \\\\\n248\t3 & .012 & .07 & $-$.06 & $-$.04 & +.06 \\\\\n249\t6 & .008 & .06 & $-$.09 & $-$.06 & +.08 \\\\\n250\t8 & .007 & .05 & $-$.10 & $-$.07 & +.10 \\\\\n251\t24 & .005 & .04 & $-$.12 & $-$.08 & +.11 \\\\\n252\t\\bottomrule\n253\t\\end{tabular}\n254\t\\caption{Convergence-divergence co-evolution on LAMBADA by attention ratio. Std: cross-architecture standard deviation. Jacc.: mean pairwise Jaccard. GDN/Ret/HGRN2: net effect on accuracy. Aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are anti-correlated ($r$=$-$0.94, $p$=0.005).}\n255\t\\label{tab:bridge}\n256\t\\end{table}\n257\t\n258\t\\subsection{Statistical Robustness}\n259\t\\label{sec:robustness}\n260\t\n261\tWe validate our findings through bootstrap confidence intervals (10,000 iterations), multiple comparison correction, effect size reporting, and sensitivity analysis excluding GatedDeltaNet (which has a known confound from the D004 configuration).\n262\t\n263\t\\paragraph{Bootstrap and Correction.}\n264\tLAMBADA's std reduction is 79\\% (95\\% CI: [72\\%, 86\\%]) with Cohen's $d$=1.84 and $\\eta^2$=0.67, a large effect. The Spearman $\\rho$=$-$1.0 (CI: [$-$1.0, $-$0.9]). After Bonferroni correction for 6 benchmarks ($\\alpha$=0.0083), LAMBADA remains significant (observed $p$=0.0083). ARC-Easy does not survive correction ($p$=0.117) but remains suggestive in the continuous spectrum framing.\n265\t\n266\t\\paragraph{GDN Exclusion Sensitivity.}\n267\tTable~\\ref{tab:gdn-exclusion} shows that all three claims survive GDN exclusion. With only RetNet, HGRN2, and Mamba2-Attn, LAMBADA $\\rho$ remains $-$1.0 (std reduction 74\\%), the scale-dependent pattern persists, and per-example divergence holds (Jaccard=0.08 for RetNet--HGRN2, with opposite effect directions).\n268\t\n269\t\\begin{table}[t]\n270\t\\centering\n271\t\\small\n272\t\\begin{tabular}{p{2.6cm}p{2cm}p{2cm}c}\n273\t\\toprule\n274\t\\textbf{Claim} & \\textbf{With GDN} & \\textbf{W/o GDN} & \\textbf{OK?} \\\\\n275\t\\midrule\n276\t1: Task-spec.\\ conv. & $\\rho$=$-$1.0, 79\\% & $\\rho$=$-$1.0, 74\\% & \\checkmark \\\\\n277\t2: Scale-dep. & 340M partial & Same pattern & \\checkmark \\\\\n278\t3: Arch-spec.\\ mech. & Jacc=0.06 & Jacc=0.08 & \\checkmark \\\\\n279\t\\bottomrule\n280\t\\end{tabular}\n281\t\\caption{GDN exclusion sensitivity. All three claims survive removal of GatedDeltaNet, confirming robustness to potential D004 confounds.}\n282\t\\label{tab:gdn-exclusion}\n283\t\\end{table}\n284\t\n285\t\\section{Comparison with Wang et al.}\n286\t\n287\tOur work builds directly on the model zoo of Wang et al.\\ \\cite{wang2025mamba}, whose core contribution is the systematic training and release of 72 hybrid models. They qualitatively observe convergence trends but provide no quantitative convergence analysis, no per-example mechanism investigation, no scale-dependent analysis, and no task-specificity characterization.\n288\t\n289\tTable~\\ref{tab:wang-comparison} summarizes the differentiation. Our contribution is orthogonal: we use their models (with proper attribution) to discover and characterize a phenomenon they only glimpsed qualitatively.\n290\t\n291\t\\begin{table}[t]\n292\t\\centering\n293\t\\small\n294\t\\begin{tabular}{p{2.1cm}p{2.2cm}p{2.7cm}}\n295\t\\toprule\n296\t\\textbf{Dimension} & \\textbf{Wang et al.} & \\textbf{This work} \\\\\n297\t\\midrule\n298\tConvergence & Qualitative & $\\rho$=$-$1.0, 79\\% std$\\downarrow$, bootstrap CI \\\\\n299\tPer-example & Not analyzed & Jaccard$<$0.10, arch-specific subsets \\\\\n300\tScale dep. & Not analyzed & Binary: 340M partial vs.\\ 1.3B complete \\\\\n301\tTask-spec. & Not analyzed & Continuous retrieval-demand spectrum \\\\\n302\t\\bottomrule\n303\t\\end{tabular}\n304\t\\caption{Systematic comparison with Wang et al.\\ \\cite{wang2025mamba}.}\n305\t\\label{tab:wang-comparison}\n306\t\\end{table}\n307\t\n308\t\\section{Discussion}\n309\t\n310\t\\paragraph{Implications for Architecture Selection.}\n311\tThe equalizer paradox suggests that aggregate benchmark comparisons between hybrid architectures may be misleading at sufficient scale. Even when two architectures achieve similar aggregate accuracy, they may handle substantially different input subsets correctly. For practitioners deploying models on specific input distributions, architecture choice remains consequential despite aggregate convergence.\n312\t\n313\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n314\tOur scale analysis is limited to a binary contrast (340M vs.\\ 1.3B); Wang et al.\\ do not provide 7B+ models, so we cannot establish a multi-point scaling trend. The strongest convergence evidence comes from a single benchmark (LAMBADA); while the continuous spectrum framing provides context, replication on additional high-retrieval tasks would strengthen the claims. Our architectural coverage, while spanning four distinct recurrence mechanisms, does not include all published hybrid designs.\n315\t\n316\t\\paragraph{Theoretical Interpretation.}\n317\tThe equalizer paradox admits a coherent mechanistic interpretation: softmax attention implements a general-purpose key-value retrieval mechanism. When combined with different recurrence cores, it learns to compensate for each core's specific weaknesses---long-range retrieval for GDN's delta rule, local disambiguation for RetNet's exponential decay, frequency-band expansion for HGRN2's hierarchical gating. This complementary compensation produces aggregate convergence through divergent per-example pathways.\n318\t\n319\t\\section{Conclusion}\n320\t\n321\tWe presented the first quantitative analysis of the equalizer effect in hybrid recurrent-attention language models. Three findings emerge: (1) attention-induced cross-architecture convergence is task-specific, scaling continuously with retrieval demand; (2) convergence is scale-dependent, requiring sufficient capacity for attention to complement rather than compete with recurrence; (3) despite aggregate convergence, per-example mechanisms are architecture-specific and largely non-overlapping. This equalizer paradox---aggregate convergence through per-example divergence---reveals that architectural differences persist beneath the surface of converging benchmarks.\n322\t\n323\t\\begin{thebibliography}{8}\n324\t\n325\t\\bibitem{wang2025mamba}\n326\tJunxiong Wang, Daniele Paliotta, Avner May, Alexander M.\\ Rush, and Tri Dao.\n327\tThe mamba in the llama: Distillation and the case for hybrid models.\n328\t{\\em arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.06457}, 2025.\n329\t\n330\t\\bibitem{yang2024gated}\n331\tSonglin Yang, Bailin Wang, Yu Zhang, Yikang Shen, and Yoon Kim.\n332\tGated delta networks: Improving mamba2 with delta rule.\n333\t{\\em arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.06464}, 2024.\n334\t\n335\t\\bibitem{sun2023retentive}\n336\tYutao Sun, Li Dong, Shaohan Huang, Shuming Ma, Yuqing Xia, Jilong Xue, Jianyong Wang, and Furu Wei.\n337\tRetentive network: A successor to transformer for large language models.\n338\t{\\em arXiv preprint arXiv:2307.08621}, 2023.\n339\t\n340\t\\bibitem{qin2024hgrn2}\n341\tZhen Qin, Songlin Yang, and Yiran Zhong.\n342\tHGRN2: Gated linear RNNs with state expansion.\n343\t{\\em arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.07904}, 2024.\n344\t\n345\t\\bibitem{dao2024transformers}\n346\tTri Dao and Albert Gu.\n347\tTransformers are SSMs: Generalized models and efficient algorithms with structured state spaces.\n348\tIn {\\em Proceedings of ICML}, 2024.\n349\t\n350\t\\bibitem{de2024griffin}\n351\tSoham De, Samuel L.\\ Smith, Anushan Fernando, Aleksandar Botev, George Cristian-Muraru, Albert Gu, Ruba Haroun, Leonard Berrada, Yutian Chen, Srivatsan Srinivasan, et al.\n352\tGriffin: Mixing gated linear recurrences with local attention for efficient language models.\n353\t{\\em arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.19427}, 2024.\n354\t\n355\t\\bibitem{srivastava2023beyond}\n356\tAarohi Srivastava, Abhinav Rastogi, Abhishek Rao, et al.\n357\tBeyond the imitation game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models.\n358\t{\\em Transactions on Machine Learning Research}, 2023.\n359\t\n360\t\\bibitem{gao2024framework}\n361\tLeo Gao, Jonathan Tow, Baber Abbasi, Stella Biderman, Sid Black, et al.\n362\tA framework for few-shot language model evaluation.\n363\t\\url{https://github.com/EleutherAI/lm-evaluation-harness}, 2024.\n364\t\n365\t\\end{thebibliography}\n366\t\n367\t\\end{document}\n368\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"bbeaa52a-3057-4860-95c0-c23758764355","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:18:27.323Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011YPCfETVN7NXPLPF6fvu1r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HGoMDLiy9yrFcojAxtjPRy","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"entrainment_gated_ft","file":"memory.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11160,"cache_read_input_tokens":29348,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11160,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"08e0ce96-c305-41bc-87d3-1d73e165ca24"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HGoMDLiy9yrFcojAxtjPRy","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/memory.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# FLA 版本与 m-a-p 模型兼容性调查结果 (2026-05-18)\\\\n\\\\n## 核心结论：fla 0.5.0 与 m-a-p 模型兼容，attn.D 丢弃无影响\\\\n\\\\n### 1. attn.D 参数真相\\\\n\\\\n- **m-a-p GatedDeltaNet 模型**（如 `1.3B-100B-GatedDeltaNet-pure`）的 checkpoint 中包含 `attn.D` 参数（每层一个，共 24 个）\\\\n- `self.D` 在 fla 的 GatedDeltaNet 初始实现（commit `4064237`, 2024-12-20）中被声明为 `nn.Parameter(torch.ones(num_heads))`，继承自 Mamba2 的 skip connection 设计\\\\n- **但 self.D 从未在 forward() 中被使用** — 它是死参数，训练过程中不参与计算、不接收梯度，始终保持初始值 `ones`\\\\n- `self.D` 在 commit `834989c` (2025-03-12) 中被移除，对应 issue #226 \\\\\\\"Remove redundant parameter\\\\\\\"\\\\n- fla v0.1.0 (2025-03-20) 起所有版本均不含 self.D\\\\n- **结论：加载模型时 attn.D 被 strict=False 静默丢弃，对模型行为和评估结果无任何影响**\\\\n\\\\n### 2. m-a-p 模型训练环境\\\\n\\\\n- **论文**: Wang et al. \\\\\\\"A Systematic Analysis of Hybrid Linear Attention\\\\\\\" (arXiv 2507.06457, July 2025)\\\\n- **训练框架**: flash-linear-attention (fla) + flame (基于 torchtitan)\\\\n- **训练数据**: FineWeb-Edu\\\\n- **模型规模**: 72 个模型（36×340M/20B tokens + 36×1.3B/100B tokens）\\\\n- **transformers 版本**: \\\\n  - 1.3B GatedDeltaNet-pure: 4.51.3 (2025-04-14)\\\\n  - RetNet / 340M New GatedDeltaNet: 4.49.0 (~2025-02)\\\\n- **fla 版本**: 未在论文或模型卡中明确标注。根据 self.D 存在于 checkpoint 推断，训练使用的是 2025-03-12 之前的 fla 开发版本（pre-v0.1.0）\\\\n- **HuggingFace 集合**: https://huggingface.co/collections/m-a-p/hybrid-linear-attention-research-686c488a63d609d2f20e2b1e\\\\n\\\\n### 3. fla PyPI 版本历史\\\\n\\\\n| 版本 | 发布日期 | 备注 |\\\\n|------|----------|------|\\\\n| 0.1.0 | 2025-03-20 | 首个 tagged release，已无 self.D |\\\\n| 0.1.1 | 2025-03-24 | |\\\\n| 0.1.2 | 2025-03-31 | |\\\\n| 0.2.0 | 2025-04-11 | |\\\\n| 0.2.1 | 2025-04-23 | |\\\\n| 0.2.2 | 2025-06-05 | GDN GVA support |\\\\n| 0.3.0 | 2025-07-14 | MesaNet/PaTH/Comba/MLA; head_first deprecated |\\\\n| 0.3.2 | 2025-09-10 | fla-core 包分离 |\\\\n| 0.4.0 | 2025-10-27 | KDA |\\\\n| 0.4.1 | 2025-12-24 | |\\\\n| 0.4.2 | 2026-03-12 | |\\\\n| 0.5.0 | 2026-04-21 | 当前版本 |\\\\n\\\\n### 4. RetNet 模型情况\\\\n\\\\n- RetNet 模型 checkpoint 中**没有 attn.D**，不存在参数丢弃问题\\\\n- config 使用 transformers 4.49.0\\\\n- 模型参数结构（q/k/v/g_proj, g_norm_swish_gate, o_proj）与 fla MultiScaleRetention 层一致\\\\n\\\\n### 5. 建议\\\\n\\\\n**使用 fla 0.5.0（或任何 >=0.1.0 版本）加载 m-a-p 模型是安全的**，只需确保加载时 `strict=False`。attn.D 被丢弃不会影响模型输出。\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# 统计报告规则 (2026-05-19, Director 硬约束)\\\\n\\\\n**所有分析报告中的统计 claim 必须附原始数值 (ρ=X, p=Y)，不允许只写结论。**\\\\n\\\\n事件：wang_et_al_differentiation.md 中 Worker 产出 \\\\\\\"4/6 benchmarks 显著收敛\\\\\\\" 的虚假 claim（实际仅 LAMBADA 1/6 显著）。Director 发现后标记为 CRITICAL。\\\\n\\\\n规则：\\\\n- 每个 \\\\\\\"显著/不显著\\\\\\\" claim 后必须跟 (ρ=X, p=Y) 或对应的检验统计量\\\\n- \\\\\\\"majority\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\"most\\\\\\\" 等量化词必须有准确计数支撑（如 \\\\\\\"4/6\\\\\\\" 必须逐一列出哪 4 个）\\\\n- 此规则适用于所有项目产出（分析报告、论文章节、汇报材料）\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# exp-003 表面特征分析教训 (2026-05-19)\\\\n\\\\n**表面特征（token frequency、context length、lexical diversity）无法区分 LAMBADA 上的 helped/hurt 样本。**\\\\n\\\\n- GDN/HGRN2：所有特征 p > 0.2，无信号\\\\n- RetNet：context_length p=0.012, r=-0.096（统计显著但效应量微不足道）；context_unique_tokens p=0.009, r=-0.099\\\\n- 跨架构比较：三架构 helped 样本特征分布无显著差异\\\\n\\\\n**启示**：per-example 效应的机制在模型内部（attention pattern / representation level），不在输入表面特征。这排除了 \\\\\\\"attention 只帮助长上下文\\\\\\\" 或 \\\\\\\"attention 只帮助低频词\\\\\\\" 等 trivial 解释，强化了 Claim 3（architecture-specific mechanism）的论证——需要 exp-004（attention weight extraction）来揭示真正的机制。\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 2882, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018BuhDkjW4o3XdCDCJFtpvP","session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"4c64d2f9-4997-4dd5-a564-af6dbc39be00","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:18:31.698Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013kD4MdwG93c37ai5iMWwk2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AgDjgzaDD3xkEguwLKdnPF","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for an EMNLP 2026 (ARR) submission. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\nScore scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments 1–5):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top ~2.5%)\n- 4.5 = Borderline Award\n- 4 = Conference (acceptable for main conference)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings (acceptable for Findings)\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Be rigorous but fair.\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Equalizer Paradox: Attention Induces Aggregate Convergence Through Per-Example Divergence in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures\"\n\nThe paper studies 4 hybrid linear recurrent architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2, Mamba2) with 5 attention ratios at 340M and 1.3B parameters. The central finding is the \"equalizer paradox\": adding attention reduces cross-architecture performance variance by up to 79% on retrieval-intensive tasks (LAMBADA), yet per-example effects are nearly non-overlapping across architectures (Jaccard &lt; 0.10).\n\nThree claims:\n1. Dose-response convergence across the retrieval spectrum (ρ=−0.89 across 6 benchmarks).\n2. Scale-dependent convergence (monotonic at 1.3B, capacity rebound at 340M).\n3. Perplexity-based mechanism: attention rescues architecture-specific high-uncertainty tokens.\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\n\n**Linear recurrent architectures.** The landscape of efficient sequence models has diversified rapidly. RetNet [?] uses multi-scale exponential decay for linear-complexity retention. HGRN2 [?] introduces hierarchical gating for state management. GatedDeltaNet applies a gated delta rule to state updates. Mamba and Mamba2 [??] use selective state-space mechanisms with input-dependent gating. Each mechanism encodes context differently, creating the preconditions for architecture-specific behavior.\n\n**Hybrid architectures.** Combining recurrent and attention layers has become standard practice. Wang et al. (2025) systematically vary the attention ratio across architectures and scales, demonstrating that even a small fraction of attention layers substantially improves downstream performance. Their model zoo (72 models across 3 architectures, 5 attention ratios, 2 scales) provides the empirical basis for our analysis. While they note aggregate convergence trends, they do not perform per-example analysis or investigate the mechanism.\n\n**Per-example analysis in NLP.** Instance-level analysis has yielded insights in machine translation [?], dataset cartography [?], and model comparison. Our use of per-example helped/hurt sets and Jaccard overlap draws on the tradition of item response theory applied to NLP benchmarks.\n\n## Key Methodological Contribution\n- The \"equalizer paradox\" concept: aggregate convergence coexisting with per-example divergence\n- Dose-response framework mapping convergence to retrieval demand\n- Perplexity-based mechanism explaining the paradox\n- Per-example helped/hurt analysis with Jaccard overlap for architecture comparison\n\n## Full Paper Structure\n1. Introduction (1.5 pages) — frames 3 claims\n2. Related Work (0.5 page) — 3 short paragraphs\n3. Experimental Setup (1 page) — models, benchmarks, analysis framework\n4. Results (4 pages) — 7 subsections covering all experiments\n5. Discussion (0.5 page) — architecture selection, ensembling, retrieval demand\n6. Limitations (0.5 page) — scale, architecture sample, benchmark, retrieval demand index, causal mechanism\n7. Conclusion (0.5 page)\n\nTables: 13 tables, no figures in the PDF.\n\n## Important Notes\n- ALL references in the PDF are broken: [?] or [??] throughout. No bibliography is present.\n- The paper uses pretrained models from Wang et al. (2025) model zoo — no new training conducted.\n- Mamba2 is mentioned as the 4th architecture from the same model zoo.\n- The \"retrieval demand index\" is \"derived from published task taxonomies\" — not operationally defined.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focused on **Novelty and Related Work**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty of the core finding**: Is the \"equalizer paradox\" genuinely novel? Is it surprising? Does it advance understanding?\n2. **Novelty of the methodology**: Per-example helped/hurt analysis, Jaccard overlap, dose-response framework — how original are these?\n3. **Positioning against prior work**: Is the paper properly situated? Are there missing references?\n4. **Related work completeness**: The section is very brief (3 short paragraphs). Is this sufficient? What's missing?\n   - Missing: model merging/ensembling literature, Simpson's paradox / ecological fallacy connections, other per-example/item-level analysis papers, other hybrid architecture papers (Jamba, Griffin, RecurrentGemma, etc.), other work on attention's role in hybrid models.\n5. **Contribution clarity**: Are the contributions clearly stated and properly differentiated from Wang et al. (2025)?\n6. **Framing quality**: Is the \"paradox\" framing justified or oversold? Is the narrative compelling?\n7. **Formatting issues**: Broken references [?], no figures, EMNLP compliance.\n\nProvide your output in this exact format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Missing References/Related Work Gaps\n[Detailed list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X (label)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34954,"cache_read_input_tokens":23646,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34954},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0a60b3f-284d-4bc2-abd7-c5a85964fe3f","uuid":"3d2ffdac-c44c-4a04-874a-63405d7642e5"}
